25240:   "note": "*** 2026-09-11: FILING A ROW WITHOUT ITS BUG-FILE ENTRY REDDENED THE GATE THREE TIMES TODAY -- ALL THREE MINE, ALL THREE THE SAME MINUTE-LATER FIX. ***\n\n`codexfarmclobber`, then `landiskintc` + `landiskboardreg`, then `agentmodelwall`.  Each time: file the ledger row, run the gate, meet `BUGFILE_SYNC_FAIL`, write the entry, green again.\n\n**The gate is working exactly as designed** -- it exists because *an OPEN row nobody can find by reading `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` is work a fresh session will not see* -- and it caught all three before any commit.  **So this is not a gate defect and not a records defect; it is a sequencing habit.**\n\n> **A NEW ROW IS TWO WRITES, NOT ONE.**  The ledger row and the bug-file entry are the same act and belong in the same script.  By the third occurrence I was predicting the red before running the check -- which is the tell that it had become a ritual rather than a lesson.\n\n*Deliberately NOT mechanised beyond what exists: the gate already refuses the commit, which is the enforceable half.  Adding a helper that writes both would be reasonable and is an owner call, not a round's -- and this project has measured that a check policing the minority of a class while alarming on prose gets switched off.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 SECOND-PARTY CHECK -- **THIS AUDIT IS THAT CHECK FOR 8 OF THE ELEVEN.  Folded in here rather than filed as a new row, because this row's own text asks for exactly this: 'the eleven reads remain owed ... pending a second party checking each one.'** ***\n\n**METHOD AND DENOMINATOR:** a subagent was given eight rows and asked to compare each row's `state` field against its actual SUBSTANCE -- what the row's body, commits and closure say happened -- rather than against its headline.  **8 of 11 checked; 3 remain owed** (`witnesssilent`, `queueunrated`, `seatunfinished` are NOT covered by this pass and their reads are still outstanding).\n\n=== THE FINDINGS, THREE DISPOSITIONS ===\n\n**CLOSEABLE NOW (3):**\n  * `stdbufasan` -- substance supports closure as it stands.\n  * `nulheredoc` -- **closeable ONCE ITS COMMIT IS NAMED.  It was missing, and is added by this pass: `25da404`, verified by `git log` before writing (*'precommit Y: guard tracked text files against NUL bytes'* -- the commit subject matches the row's subject exactly).**  A row that shipped a guard and names no commit is the state-vs-substance defect in its purest form: the record says nothing landed and something did.\n  * `hpcshcpu` -- closeable, but **as `dropped`, NOT `closed`**.  *This is the `es438` shape this project already recorded: a state that misdescribes the work.  There the error ran the other way -- `dropped` stamped on shipped, witnessed, reviewed work, which a future reader trusting states reads as 'not done'.*  **State change deliberately NOT applied in this pass**, because a bug-file sync audit is in flight against the same file and two writers to one record is how this project produced `BUGFILE_SYNC_FAIL` three times.  *A new row is two writes, not one* -- and so is a closure.\n\n**CLOSEABLE ONCE X (3):** `smdatefmt` (commits `6e36943`, verified), `idesync`, `pitclobber` -- each has a named precondition rather than an open question.\n\n**NOT CLOSEABLE (2):**\n  * `smnotland` -- substance does not support closure.\n  * `wdcoracle` -- **and this one is worth its own sentence: the quoted phrase the row relies on sits DIRECTLY BELOW ITS OWN REFUTATION in the same field.**  A reader who greps for the phrase lands on the claim; a reader who reads the paragraph lands on the refutation.  *That is the stale-text-grep collision this project already records -- a naive search cannot tell an ASSERTION from a QUOTATION OF ONE -- appearing inside a row rather than inside the carrier.*\n\n=== CROSS-CUTTING, AND THE THIRD ITEM IS A LIVE CHECK-THAT-CANNOT-FAIL ===\n  * **0 of 8 carry live HELD markers** -- so none of these is waiting on a seat, and the held-marker machinery is not what is holding them.\n  * **5 of 5 cited commit hashes are REAL**, re-verified here by `git log --oneline -1` on each (`25da404`, `020a672`, `6e36943`, `0922465`) rather than taken from the audit.\n  * *** `pitclobber`'s witness reads `none needed -- the defect was fixed by #439 (020a672), which also shipped regress/pit8253_latch_probe.py (308 lines) as its detector.`  **That string MECHANICALLY PASSES `check_witness.py`'s `none` regex while being doctrinally wrong: it describes, in its own words, exactly what the `detector:<path>` term was adopted on 2026-08-27 to express.** ***  The checker cannot fail on it -- the field opens with the one token the regex accepts, and everything after it is prose the regex never reads.  *So the conflation `detector:` was adopted to END is still standing in at least this row, and the term's arrival did not migrate the rows that motivated it.*\n\n**A WIDER SWEEP WAS RUN ON THAT LAST POINT RATHER THAN LEFT AS A SUGGESTION.**  Across all 461 rows, **164 carry a `witness` beginning `none`; of those, 16 ALSO NAME A COMMIT** -- `a1a2`, `asiceventaddr`, `carrier`, `census`, `constblind`, `fablequeue`, `gdromdmawidth`, `m8batc`, `mrwstore2`, `ns16550`, `ovsync`, `panelseatlabel`, `pcc2genctlc040alias`, `pitclobber`, `probewiring`, `wdccdbmidxfer`.  **Most are legitimately `none`** (records-only rounds, policy rows, and honest 'none yet' fields).  *An adversarial audit of all sixteen is in flight; the disposition proposed to it is FORWARD-ONLY -- only rows still `open`/`in work`/`held` are actionable, because a rule that retro-fails a large population is a rule that gets switched off, which is worse than a warning.*\n\n*Provenance: the check was run by a SONNET subagent while both Anthropic seats were walled -- see [[agentmodelwall]].  It is a READING, and it is scored as one.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 ID-IN-SOURCE SWEEP, ADJUDICATED (Opus 5, read-only).  **A = 0, B = 19, C = 3 -- THE SWEEP'S ONE REAL HIT WAS THE ONE I HAD ALREADY VERIFIED BY HAND.** ***\n\n**THE METHOD.**  Live rows (`open`/`in work`/`held`) with an EMPTY `commits` field whose id appears in COMMITTED `src/` -- **23 of 284**.  The hypothesis was that some are fixes that shipped without the row being updated, as [[m88kexcdeliver]] was.\n\n**THE RESULT: of the 22 sent for classification, ZERO are shipped fixes.**  19 are **(B) the round's own disclosure of the defect it declined to fix**, 3 are **(C) the row appearing as a third party in a comment about something else**.  *No `commits:` is owed by any of them and no state should change.*\n\n=== THE DISCRIMINATOR, AND IT IS MECHANICAL ===\n**`m88kexcdeliver` has a shape none of the 22 share: SEVEN tag-on-CODE-LINE hits** -- `implemented = true;  /*  m88kexcdeliver  */`.  Across the 22, **30 unique committed hit lines (31 grep results; `ppcrldimimask` and `ppcrlwimiwidth` SHARE line 2493), and 0 of 30 contain an assignment, guard, constant or branch.**  Every one is prose inside a `/* */`.\n\nEvery B carries an explicit NON-FIX verb, which is what makes the class readable at a glance: *filed / tracked / recorded / not taken here / not reopened here / not this round's / still lands here by design / STILL halt / still not implemented / stays filed / standing do-not-code ruling*.  **These are the ledger working as intended -- a round closing its scope and naming what it left.**\n\n*** THE WORKTREE-VS-HEAD CAUTION WAS NOT HYPOTHETICAL AND IT WOULD HAVE COST TWO LIVE ROWS. ***\nThe brief required `git grep ... HEAD` rather than a worktree grep.  Measured difference:\n\n| file | row | HEAD hits | worktree hits |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `dev_lca.c` | `lcabadval` | 1 | **2** -- the extra is my in-flight `#518` tag |\n| `dev_sgi_re.c` | `sgirandomcolour` | 1 | **3** -- two extra `#519` tags |\n| `cpu_ppc_instr.c` | both PPC rows | 1 each | 1 each |\n\n**`#518` and `#519` appear ZERO times in `src/` at HEAD.**  *Had the sweep read the worktree, `lcabadval` (HIGH) and `sgirandomcolour` (HIGH) would BOTH have classified as shipped fixes -- marking two live rows done on the strength of my own uncommitted edits.*  That is the measure-your-own-in-flight-work-as-evidence trap, and the only thing that stopped it was naming the tree.\n\n=== THE HONEST BOUND ON MY OWN METHOD, which is the part worth keeping ===\n> **An id-in-source sweep finds only fixes that TAG THE ROW ID.  A shipped fix tagged only `/* #NNN */` is INVISIBLE to it.**  So *23 of 284* is a **LOWER BOUND** on shipped-but-unrecorded, the yield is **1 real in 23 hits (~4%)**, and **the actual staleness risk sits in the 261 rows with no id in source, where this method reports nothing either way.**\n\n*That is the denominator rule turned on a sweep of mine: `0 of 0` and `0 of 41` are different claims, and so are `1 of 23 checked` and `1 of 284`.  The sweep is worth running and is not worth trusting as coverage.*\n\n**THREE SUB-SHAPES OF B, recorded because they look like staleness and are not:**\n  * **residual-of-a-shipped-fix** (`m88kexcsfu1`, `m88kexctaildefault`, `wdcconsumedattempts`, `pcc2pscaleflood`) -- a fix shipped nearby and the row IS the uncovered remainder, said so in the same comment;\n  * **held-shut-by-a-shipped-guard** ([[sgilinearoob]]) -- `#497`'s `len != 8` guard mitigates the OOB without fixing it, and the comment says the fix *'deliberately does NOT add a clamp'* because the row is filed to fix all five arms with ONE design.  *My hypothesis about this row was right and my count was wrong: it is **6 lines across two MIRRORED arms**, not one.*\n  * **made-reachable-by-a-shipped-fix** ([[m88kdslotshadow]]) -- `#502` turned a latent upstream arm into an ordinary-fault path, so the row is open BECAUSE of the fix.\n\n*** AND A PRIOR INSTANCE OF THE CLASS I WAS SWEEPING FOR WAS FOUND ALREADY SELF-CORRECTED: *** `m88kexctaildefault`'s own note records that its `cpu_m88k.c:796` citation **predates the row** -- *'This row was CITED by #502's product comment and CHANGELOG as if it existed before it did ... filed 2026-09-06 to make the citation true.'*  *A round citing a row id that does not yet exist is the same records defect from the other end, and that one was caught and fixed by its own author.*\n\n**Corroboration for work in flight:** the classification independently confirmed both PowerPC defects are STILL LIVE AT HEAD rather than trusting their rows -- `X(rldimi)` still has `while (sh-- != 0)` feeding `while (m != 63 - sh)`, and `X(rlwimi)` still rotates a `MODE_uint_t` with no `uint32_t` truncation.  *Those are the two the uncommitted diff repairs, and a pass 2 on it is in flight.*"
34667:   "state_note": "[2026-09-19] SHIPPED as #518 (a616d20, 2026-09-12).  Awaiting its regress/closure stage (Fable-only by standing directive).  *** RECORDS CORRECTION 2026-09-06, from RE-DERIVING this row's own citations instead of trusting them.  THE\nHEADLINE COUNT (eleven live terminating calls) IS RIGHT.  THE ENUMERATION UNDER IT IS WRONG IN TWO WAYS, AND THE\nFIRST ONE CHANGES THE FIX DESIGN. ***\n\n**1.  `dev >= 21` IS NOT A LIVE KILL.  IT IS ALREADY DE-FATALISED -- AND IT IS THIS FILE'S OWN FIX PRECEDENT.**\n\nThe row lists it among \"two range guards that also kill\".  The source says otherwise: the guard is at `:127`, its\nbody is a COMMENTED-OUT `fatal()` + `exit(1)` at `:128-129`, and what actually executes is `dev = 0;` at `:130`.\n\n```c\n\tif (dev >= 21) {\n\t\t/*  fatal(\"[ LCA: No bus 0 device? TODO ]\\n\");\n\t\texit(1);  */\n\t\tdev = 0;\n\t}\n```\n\nSomeone has already made, in this very file, the decision this row exists to make: **delete the kill, substitute a\nsafe default, keep going.**  That is a TRANSFERRED WARRANT from inside the same file, which the six-member\noption-set rule says to go looking for -- *\"grep the fork for an existing idiom; the tree's own precedents are part\nof the design space\"* -- and which nobody had found because the row was read rather than re-derived.  So only ONE\nrange guard is live: `ofs > len` at `:163`.\n\n**2.  THE ENUMERATION COVERED 8 OF THE 11, AND THE THREE IT MISSED ARE NOT VALUE GUARDS AT ALL.**\n\nRe-derived by `grep -n 'exit(1)' dev_lca.c` minus the commented one (12 matches, 11 live), the eleven are:\n\n| line | class | condition |\n|---|---|---|\n| 73  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_assert` -- bare `fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1)`, **NO GUARD** |\n| 85  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_deassert` -- likewise |\n| 134 | BUS | `bus != 0`, i.e. `d->ioc_conf & 1` |\n| 163 | RANGE | `ofs > len` |\n| 206 | VALUE | `idata & ~1` (IOC_CONF) |\n| 226 | VALUE | `idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` |\n| 241 | VALUE | `idata != 0 && idata != 0x300800000` |\n| 256 | VALUE | `idata != 0x700000` |\n| 280 | VALUE | `idata != 0x240000000` |\n| 295 | VALUE | `idata != 0x3ff00000` |\n| 315 | CATCH-ALL | `default:` -- any unimplemented ioc offset, read OR write |\n\n**THE TWO INTERRUPT STUBS ARE PLAUSIBLY THE MOST SEVERE OF THE ELEVEN, AND A ROW FRAMED AS A VALUE-GUARD CLASS\nCOULD NOT SEE THEM.**  They are not a wrong value being written -- they are an ENTIRE UNIMPLEMENTED PATH with no\npredicate at all, so *any* device asserting an interrupt through the LCA ends the process regardless of what it\nwrites.  The four \"accepts exactly one value\" registers need a firmware revision that writes something else; these\ntwo need only that an interrupt is asserted.\n\n*A row named after one class hides the members of the others.  That is this project's cell-not-class pattern\nwearing an enumeration instead of a detector -- the same shape the m88kexcdeliver chain hit four times.*\n\n**WHAT IS UNCHANGED:** the severity (HIGH), the single consumer (`machine_alpha.c`, `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`),\nthe absence of any alpha rig, and the observation that a guest here is FIRMWARE so the reaching value is whatever\nSRM or NetBSD writes.  The scope caveat about range-vs-value guards also stands and is now sharper, because there\nis only one live range guard to reason about.\n\n--- the original note follows, unaltered ---\n\n\n=== DESIGN PASS, 2026-09-06 (codex / GPT-6 Astra at max, read-only).  Answer preserved at\n=== regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_design_20260906.txt.  IT CORRECTED THREE OF THE FIVE PREMISES I HAD\n=== JUST RE-DERIVED, and one of the corrections changes what this round may even choose from.\n\n**SEVERITY ORDER CONFIRMED: the two INTERRUPT STUBS first** -- but \"provisionally; firmware execution order is\nunproved\", which is the honest qualifier my own framing lacked.\n\n*** A SEVENTH OPTION-SET MEMBER: IMPLEMENT. ***  The carrier's set is DELETE / DECLINE / DECLINE+LATCHED\nANNOUNCE / ACCEPT / FAULT / BACKED ACCEPT -- all six presume the access is a REFUSAL DECISION.  For the two\ninterrupt stubs none of them applies: *\"declining an interrupt leaves the machine incomplete.\"*  A stub is not a\nrefusal to service a value; it is an unimplemented PATH, and the disposition is to write the code.  This is the\noption-set completeness rule paying off exactly as it is meant to -- the member was found by a seat reading the\ntree, not by argument.\n\n*** AND THE FAULT MEMBER IS NOT AVAILABLE ON THIS MACHINE AT ALL. ***  `dev_unreadable.c` works because\n`memory_rw.c` dispatches an exception on a failed device access **for MIPS and M88K, with NO ALPHA EQUIVALENT**.\nSo the idiom this fork installs ten times in `machine_sgi.c` does not transfer here, and any round reaching for\nFAULT owes Alpha exception delivery first.  *A precedent that is real elsewhere in the tree is not thereby\ntransferable; that is the warrant-transfer rule, and it took a seat opening `memory_rw.c` to see it.*\n\n**AND A FINDING ABOUT THE ALPHA PORT THAT OUTRANKS THIS ROW:** the Alpha interrupt callbacks merely set\n`irq_asserted`, and **the execution loop never consumes that flag**.  So implementing the two stubs would not by\nitself deliver an interrupt -- removing the exits cannot complete the path.  Filed as its own question rather\nthan folded in here.\n\n## THE PREMISE AUDIT, and I was wrong three times\n\n* **PREMISE 1 HOLDS.**  Twelve textual occurrences, eleven outside comments.\n* **PREMISE 2's REMOVAL IS REAL; ITS \"USABLE PRECEDENT\" CONCLUSION IS WRONG.**  I read the commented-out\n  `dev >= 21` kill plus its live `dev = 0` as the file's own answer, transferable to this round.  It is not: it\n  **ALIASES \"no selected device\" to DEVICE ZERO**, which is harmless today only because device 7 is the sole\n  installed device and slot 0 happens to be empty.  Populate slot 0 and it is a latent alias defect.  *So the\n  question I asked -- is this a sound precedent or a latent defect of its own -- has the second answer, and my\n  brief had already leaned on the first.*\n* **PREMISE 3's CLASS COUNTS HOLD, ITS SINGLETON CLAIM DOES NOT.**  `W_BASE0` accepts **TWO** values, `0` and\n  `0x300800000` (`idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL`), so \"four registers accepting exactly ONE value\" is\n  wrong -- only **three** window guards accept a single value.  *This one is mine twice over: the original row\n  note said it, I re-derived the enumeration tonight and reproduced it, and the guard's own text was in my\n  terminal when I did.*\n* **PREMISE 4 HOLDS** as one machine-setup consumer (AlphaBook 1), with no dedicated boot rig -- **but\n  \"never exercised\" DOES NOT FOLLOW**: a generic constructor sweep exists (`gate_asan_sweep.sh`), without\n  proving coverage of these branches.  My \"no rig means no coverage\" was too strong.\n* **PREMISE 5 IS AN UNSUPPORTED RESTRICTION.**  I said a guest here is firmware, so the reaching value is\n  whatever SRM or NetBSD writes.  Guest KERNEL stores use the same MMIO path, and interrupt callbacks arise from\n  DEVICE events -- neither is firmware.\n\n## DISPOSITIONS IT WOULD TAKE, per class\n* **INTERRUPT STUB (2):** IMPLEMENT forwarding and CPU interrupt delivery.\n* **BUS (1):** implement Type-1 decoding then ACCEPT through `bus_pci`, using its existing absent-device\n  handling.  `ioc_conf & 1` selects the CONFIGURATION-CYCLE TYPE and is **not the decoded bus number** -- and\n  deleting this exit alone leaves `dev` uninitialised.\n* **RANGE (1):** DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE with initialised read results and no forwarded side effects.  Note\n  `ofs` is at most three, **so this guard cannot fire for four- or eight-byte accesses** -- changing `>`\n  mechanically is not a design.\n* **VALUE (6):** ACCEPT documented register fields using the existing backing, latched announce for the missing\n  DMA/TLB effects.  **Every guarded assignment already happens BEFORE its check**, so no invented backing is\n  needed -- but readback alone does not implement translation.\n* **CATCH-ALL (1):** split documented registers (`STAT0`/`STAT1`) from genuine holes; FAULT only for confirmed\n  holes and only once Alpha exception delivery exists.\n\n**WHAT IT COULD NOT ESTABLISH, said plainly:** source shows connected interrupt paths and conditional MMIO paths\nfor the other nine sites, but **no particular SRM/NetBSD execution of any terminating site**.  Firmware\nreachability and failure frequency remain unmeasured, so the severity order is provisional on exactly that.\n\n\n*** THE SEVERITY WARRANT THIS ROW WAS MISSING: THE REJECTED VALUES INCLUDE A DOCUMENTED ONE, AND THE\nSTORE LANDS BEFORE THE PROCESS ENDS.  Found by the codex seat 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt),\nverified against the source and the header before recording. ***\n\nEach of the three window guards has this shape:\n\n    d->window_mask_0 = idata;                          <- the store LANDS\n    if (idata != 0x700000ULL) { fatal(...); exit(1); } <- then the process ends\n\n**And zero is a documented encoding.**  From src/include/thirdparty/alpha_lcareg.h:\n\n    #define IOC_W_MASK_1M   0x0000000000000000ULL   /* 1MB window */\n\nSo a guest selecting the SMALLEST DOCUMENTED WINDOW SIZE ends the emulator.  The same argument covers\nW_BASE1, whose one accepted constant 0x240000000 carries IOC_W_BASE_WEN (0x200000000, *Window enable*) --\n**so a driver DISABLING a window, by writing a base with WEN clear, is killed too.**\n\n*** THIS IS #504's WARRANT AT A SECOND DEVICE: documented values that the model kills on. ***  It is not a\njunk-value guard; it is a whitelist of ONE that excludes documented members of its own encoding.  codex adds\nthat all three registers start at zero through constructor initialisation, so the rejected value is also the\nRESET value.\n\n**AND THE RANGE SITE IS FAR MORE REACHABLE THAN THIS ROW SAID.**  It was recorded as `ofs > len'.  Reading\nDEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa): `relative_addr >>= 5; ofs = relative_addr & 3;' -- **ofs is guest ADDRESS BITS 5-6**,\nnot a written value.  So an ordinary 1-byte access at an address with those bits set to 2 or 3 ends the\nprocess.  That is address decoding, not a value whitelist, and it belongs in a different disposition class\nfrom the six value guards.  The rest of that function is filed separately as [[lcaisalane]].\n\n*** DISPOSITION PASS, codex 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_disposition_20260907.txt).  IT NARROWS MY REACHABILITY CLAIM,\nCONFIRMS TWO OTHERS, AND SUPPLIES THE IN-TREE PRECEDENTS THE OPTION-SET RULE ASKS FOR -- INCLUDING\nCONFLICTING ONES, WHICH IS WHAT I ASKED IT TO SURFACE. ***\n\nVerdict: *\"Disposition 5 is unavailable through Alpha's failed-device-access path.  Five candidate rounds fit\na uniform VALUE-refusal policy.  The tree supplies CONFLICTING precedents, especially for lane handling and\nstoring unsupported values.\"*\n\n*** MY `AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE ENDS THE EMULATOR' CLAIM IS TOO BROAD, AND I HAVE REPEATED IT IN THREE\nPLACES. ***  codex: *\"they read zero INITIALLY, then echo accepted writes.  Writing back the initial zero\nkills the last three; an arbitrary RMW does not necessarily kill them -- its resulting value matters.\"*\n\n**The precise claim is the INITIAL-ZERO readback**: before any accepted write, `W_MASK0', `W_BASE1' and\n`W_MASK1' read 0 and accept only one non-zero constant each, so a read-modify-write performed on a\nfreshly-reset device writes 0 back and ends the process.  **Once a valid value has been written the register\nechoes it**, and an RMW of that value is accepted.  So the window is early -- driver probe or\ninitialisation -- rather than any RMW at any time.  That is still a routine thing for a driver to do at\nattach, and it is still reached with a documented encoding (`IOC_W_MASK_1M` = 0), but the sentence I wrote\nwas wider than the fact.\n\n**CONFIRMED, both of which I had asserted:** every VALUE arm STORES BEFORE CHECKING; and the accepted sets\nare exactly as filed (`IOC_CONF' {0,1}, `IOC_TB_ENA' {0,0x80}, `W_BASE0' {0,0x300800000}, and the three\nsingle-value ones).\n\n**DISPOSITION 5 IS UNAVAILABLE -- now established from a third direction.**  The carrier already recorded it\ngenerally, [[alphafaultclaim]] reached it from the instruction side, and this reaches it from the\nfailed-device-access path.  **Any proposal for a bus-error refusal on an Alpha device owes Alpha exception\ndelivery first**, which is a far larger round than the one it would be riding on.\n\n**THE PRECEDENT I ASKED FOR, AND IT IS AN EXACT ONE:** `cpu_sh.c' defines `sh3_cpu_interrupt_assert()' and\n`sh3_cpu_interrupt_deassert()' with EMPTY `/* TODO */' bodies -- *\"an exact unimplemented-callback precedent\nfor 2, ignore silently\"* -- and `core/interrupt.c' defines empty `no_interrupt_assert/deassert' callbacks,\nthough those represent intentionally absent connections rather than unimplemented ones.  **So the two\nINTERRUPT STUBS have a direct in-tree answer** and do not need disposition 7 after all, which is what I had\nassumed they needed.\n\n**AND IT REPORTS THE PRECEDENTS CONFLICT** -- especially on lane handling and on whether to store an\nunsupported value.  That is the more useful answer: it means the choice is genuinely open at those sites\nrather than settled by the tree, so a round there must argue rather than transfer.\n\n**SCOPE: FIVE candidate rounds** under a uniform VALUE-refusal policy -- consistent with the earlier read's\n*\"this needs several rounds\"* and finer than it.\n\n**WHERE THE OVER-BROAD FORM SURVIVES, named because it cannot be edited:** commit `de96f11`'s MESSAGE\nsays *\"each reads back 0 while its guard accepts one non-zero constant, and AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE\nENDS THE EMULATOR on all three\"*.  A commit message is immutable, so the house rule -- correct the false\nrecord IN PLACE -- cannot be followed literally.  **The next best thing is to point at it from the record a\nreader will reach**, which is this row.  The narrowed claim above is the operative one: the kill needs the\nINITIAL zero readback, not any RMW at any time.\n\n*** THE PREREQUISITE IS DISCHARGED.  `#506` LANDED THIS AFTERNOON. ***\n\nThis row's HOLD-Q asked: *\"given that every row must match the `fatal()` TEXT rather than the exit\nstatus, and given that `lcaconfwidth` means the IOC_CONF arm's text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the\ntext-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must `lcaconfwidth`'s printf fix land FIRST, making it a\nprerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?\"*\n\n**It landed.**  `#506` repaired both narrowing sites in `dev_lca.c` -- `:205` (IOC_CONF) and the\n`default:` arm -- to `0x%\" PRIx64` with `(uint64_t)idata`, matching the five siblings.  Measured by\n`regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py`: **HEAD 4/6 -> fix 6/6, same script**, with `kill width` now reading\n`0x100000000` instead of `0x0`.\n\n> **So the text oracle is now sound at the IOC_CONF arm, and the ordering question in the HOLD-Q is\n> answered by events rather than by adjudication.**  What remains of that HOLD-Q is its SECOND half,\n> untouched: *the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger's earlier\n> census dropped `W_BASE1` and `W_MASK1` -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four.*\n\n*The interrupt-stub confound is also unchanged and still governs the witness design:\n`lca_interrupt_assert` and `lca_interrupt_deassert` are bare `fatal()`+`exit(1)` with the ISA PIC\nchain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the same status as the value guards under\ntest.  That is why the rows must match text, which is why `#506` was the prerequisite.*\n\n*** THE HOLD-Q'S SECOND HALF IS ANSWERED BY MEASUREMENT: THE ACCEPT-ROW SET IS SIX. ***\nEnumerated by reading `if (idata` in `dev_lca.c`, 2026-09-07:\n\n    :203  IOC_CONF   idata & ~1\n    :231  TB_ENA     idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN\n    :246  W_BASE0    idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL\n    :261  W_MASK0    idata != 0x700000ULL\n    :285  W_BASE1    idata != 0x240000000ULL\n    :300  W_MASK1    idata != 0x3ff00000ULL\n\n**Six, not four** -- the earlier census in this row dropped `W_BASE1` and `W_MASK1`, exactly as the\nHOLD-Q suspected.  *Note two shapes, which may matter for the fix design: two BITMASK tests\n(`& ~mask`) and four EQUALITY tests, one of which accepts two values.*\n\n**And the eleven-live count is confirmed rather than carried:** `grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'`\ngives **12**, of which `:129` sits inside a commented-out block -- so 11 live, in **FIVE** classes:\n2 interrupt stubs, 1 bus/cycle-type, 1 range/lane, 6 value, 1 default.\n\n> #### *** THIS SENTENCE SAID \"four classes\" AND THEN LISTED FIVE.  Corrected 2026-09-07 after the\n> #### grok design pass named it -- and the same slip was in the brief that asked it to. ***\n> The opener asks seats to check enumerations; mine was wrong in its own headline, with the\n> refutation printed three words later.  *A count and the list beside it are two claims, and\n> nothing checks them against each other.*\n\n*** AND THE FIRST HALF OF THE HOLD-Q IS DISCHARGED BY EVENTS: `#506` LANDED (`7a41b3f`). ***\nThe question was whether `lcaconfwidth`'s printf fix had to land first, since this row's rows must\nmatch the `fatal()` TEXT rather than the exit status (the two interrupt stubs kill with the same\nstatus as every value guard).  **It landed and is measured** -- both narrowing sites now print\n`0x%\" PRIx64` with `(uint64_t)idata`, HEAD 4/6 -> fix 6/6 -- so the IOC_CONF arm's text is sound.\n\n**Design brief out to grok** (`regress/artifacts/lcabadval/DESIGN_brief.md`) rather than codex: the\ncodex seat refused the sibling `lcaconfwidth` census on its content filter, and a brief enumerating\neleven host kills is the same recorded trigger shape.\n\n*** GROK DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07: **NOT RATIFY** as a six-armed one-design round until two of my\nclaims are dropped.  The ROUND SHAPE survives; two warrants and one count do not. ***\n\n**1. \"THE ACCEPT-ROW SET IS SIX\" STILL UNDER-COUNTS.**  De-escalation owes a row per PREDICATE, but\nthree of the six predicates accept TWO values:\n\n    IOC_CONF  {0, 1}          TB_ENA   {0, 0x80}      W_BASE0  {0, 0x300800000}\n    W_MASK0   {0x700000}      W_BASE1  {0x240000000}  W_MASK1  {0x3ff00000}\n\n**Nine pinning values, not six rows** -- and three of the nine are the reset `0`, which is vacuous\nunless sequenced after a nonzero write.  *So replacing the old \"four\" with \"six\" corrected one\nunder-count into another.*\n\n**2. S5 DOES NOT TRANSFER, AND CITING IT WOULD CITE A DEFECT AS A WARRANT.**  I offered the live\n`dev = 0` path (`dev >= 21`, kill commented out) as this file's own *delete-the-kill, substitute-a-\nsafe-default* precedent.  **It is PCI type-0 TAG DECODE, not a register-value policy**: it aliases\n\"no device selected\" onto SLOT 0, which is empty today only because the installed SIO is device 7.\n**And that loop is already filed as [[lcashiftub]] -- `0x800 << 20` in a signed `int` at `dev == 20`\nis UB.**  Substituting `0x700000` when firmware wrote `IOC_W_MASK_1M` (0) would be that same alias\npointed at a documented encoding.\n\n> **THE RIGHT IN-FILE WARRANT IS `W_T_BASE0` / `W_T_BASE1`**: the sibling TRANSLATED-window registers\n> already store any value and do not kill.  *Base/mask killing on a firmware snapshot while\n> translated-base accepts everything is the file's own outlier* -- which is a far stronger warrant\n> than the one I proposed, and one grep away.\n\n**3. THE ROUND SHAPE IS RATIFIED: ONE ROUND, MEMBER 4 ACCEPT, ALL SIX.**  Delete `fatal`+`exit` and\nkeep the store that already ran.  Five of the six fields are pure storage (`window_base_*`,\n`window_mask_*`, `tlb_enable` appear only as `odata = d->X` / `d->X = idata`); the sixth, `ioc_conf`,\nis consumed only as `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` in `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`, **so extra CONF bits\nare already discarded at the only consumer** and a masked store would be an unlicensed addition.\n**Bitmask vs equality is a DETECTOR-SHAPE split, not a fix-design split.**  FAULT is unavailable on\nAlpha.\n\n*** 4. THE FLOOR-(a) TRAP, and it is the finding that most changes the detector: AN ACCEPT-ONLY\nDETECTOR IS GREEN ON HEAD. ***  Writing the six magic constants SURVIVES on the committed build.  So\na detector that writes only currently-legal values **is green under verbatim reinstatement of all six\noriginal guards** -- the vacuity-floor miss in its purest form.  **The rows that make (b) bite are the\ndocumented-REJECTED writes**: MASK0 `0`, BASE1 with WEN clear, CONF bit 32, a non-TEN TB_ENA bit.\n\n**5. THE (d) FORMS HERE ARE SITE OMISSION AND DISJUNCT-DROPPING, not an operand swap.**  Leave\n`W_MASK1` (or `W_BASE1`) fatal while de-fatalising the other five -- incomplete clause (b), and\n**`ioc rt` in `lca_confwidth_probe.py` stays green because nothing in that probe touches MASK1.**\nSecond form: on `W_BASE0`, drop `idata != 0ULL &&` and keep the magic comparison -- the magic accept\nrow stays green, *which is exactly why BASE0 owes TWO pins*.\n\n**6. `lca_confwidth_probe.py`'s `kill fired` and `died` WANTS WILL INVERT IF THIS ROUND SHIPS.**  It\nis a `#506` witness, not this round's detector.  **Re-pin it under the oracle-correction rule -- do\nnot launder by editing wants after the product change.**\n\n**7. THE STUBS ARE A SIBLING, NOT A PREREQUISITE.**  Construction does not fire them today (PIC\n`ier`/`irr` start 0, 8253 `in_use=0`, WDC only with an IDE image, ns16550 needs `com_ier` bits), and\n`#506`'s legal session already survived on this machine.  **Text is sufficient to ATTRIBUTE a kill\nand is NOT a survival oracle** -- an accept row wanting `alive` still false-reds if a stub fires, and\nthat is controlled by SESSION SHAPE (cold prompt, `step`, don't touch the ISA window), not by\nde-fatalising the stubs first.  *A BOOT witness would make them a prerequisite, because `timer_stop`\nis why stepping does not tick the PIT.*\n\n**8. AND AN HONESTY CONSTRAINT FOR THE CONTRACT:** `default:` still kills `STAT0`/`STAT1`, which the\nheader documents as R/W1C.  **This round can be green and firmware can still die**, so the contract\nmust name that as out of scope or the round will be reported as a false pass of *\"LCA no longer\nhost-kills on IOC MMIO\"*.\n\n**Split out, each its own round:** the interrupt stubs (member 7); `bus != 0` -- *deleting that\n`exit(1)` leaves `dev` UNINITIALISED in the `else`*, which is the sketch rule and not a rider;\n`ofs > len` (already [[lcaisalane]]; `ofs` is address bits 5-6); and the `default:` arm.  **HAE at\n`case LCA_IOC_BASE` already silently ignores writes to a documented register -- that is member 2, not\n4, and must not be unified with the six.**\n\n*** 2026-09-07, CODEX FIX-CHECK: QUALIFIED YES, AND IT FOUND A COUNTEREXAMPLE TO THE ROUND'S CENTRAL WARRANT. ***  All six stores confirmed prior to their guards (table, full denominator); the consumer analysis confirmed with one sharpening -- masking CONF would change READBACK policy, not merely bus selection.  **But `dev_sh4.c's RCR1 comment records that its store USED TO run before the exit and was DELIBERATELY MOVED BELOW THE GUARD**, so \"the store already ran\" is not a general warrant in this tree.  The round now carries an open A-vs-B design question (keep the store vs decline the write) that the W_T_BASE* siblings CANNOT settle, since accepting-anything there may be an absent guard rather than a decision.  Disposition set also grew by two source-backed members (accept/decline WITH REPEATED announcements; complete with a GUEST-VISIBLE DEVICE ERROR, dev_wdc.c's WDCE_ABRT+interrupt).  And a correction to my own brief: only TWO BITS IN STAT0 are R/W1C, not STAT1.\n\n*** TITLE CORRECTED 2026-09-07: it said FOUR registers and the true count is SIX. *** Derived, not recalled: `exit(1)` occurs SEVEN times in DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) -- six guarded value-arms (LCA_IOC_CONF :215, TB_ENA :235, W_BASE0 :250, W_MASK0 :265, W_BASE1 :289, W_MASK1 :304) and the `default:` arm at :324, which this round does NOT close.  The four window registers are presumably what the row was opened on; CONF and TB_ENA carry the same shape and were folded in later without the title following.  **A stale title is how a queue row is READ** -- the brief that goes to the panel says six, and a reader comparing the two would have had to guess which was right.  W_T_BASE0 (:272) and W_T_BASE1 (:311) confirmed UNGUARDED: plain readback/store, no value test -- which is the sibling warrant the design brief puts to the panel.\n\n*** AND THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE WAS ITSELF CORRUPTED WHEN FIRST WRITTEN -- BY THE SHELL, QUIETLY\nENOUGH TO SURVIVE REVIEW. ***  It was applied through `python -c \"...\"` with DOUBLE quotes, and\nbash performed COMMAND SUBSTITUTION on the backticks inside it: `exit(1)` and `default:` were\nEXECUTED and replaced by their empty output, leaving readable prose with two words silently\nmissing -- \"Derived, not recalled:  occurs SEVEN times\".  bash did print two errors, but python\nran on the mangled source anyway and the write SUCCEEDED, so the record was wrong while the\ncommand reported success.\n\n**Inside a double-quoted shell argument a backtick is a COMMAND, not a quote mark.**  That is the\ncarrier's backslash-through-heredoc class arriving on a new character, and with the same\nsignature: the failure lands in the FILE, not in the exit status.\n\n*** AND THE REPAIR FAILED TWICE MORE BEFORE IT LANDED, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. ***  The\nfix was retried as `python -c '...'` with SINGLE quotes -- correct for backticks -- and bash\nrejected it, because the note explaining the hazard contains the word \"carrier's\", whose\nAPOSTROPHE terminated the shell quote.  So: double quotes lose to backticks, single quotes lose to\napostrophes, and English prose about shell quoting reliably contains both.\n\n**The rule is not a better quoting style.  It is: PROSE DESTINED FOR A RECORD GOES INTO A SCRIPT\nFILE, never through `-c`.**  This paragraph was written that way and landed intact on the first\nattempt.\n\n*** PASS-1 DESIGN PANEL 2026-09-07 (preserved: regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_p1_20260907/ -- _scratchpad is never committed).  PROVISIONAL: FIVE OF THE\nLANDED SEATS ARE READ HERE AND **CODEX WAS STILL STREAMING** WHEN THIS WAS WRITTEN (its file grew\n14,943 -> 30,594 bytes between two reads and its tail is mid-`exec`, not a verdict).  Any ruling\nshort of the roster is provisional on its face, so no disposition is adopted below. ***\n\nSEATS: grok, glm, deepseek, minimax and the kimi-k3:cloud SUBSTITUTE answered (5); codex IN FLIGHT;\nthe kimi CLI WALLED on an explicit 403 weekly limit, so that seat is COVERED, not lost; agy\nDELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (measurement reserve, owner ruling 2026-08-30); opus5 and fable5 NOT FIRED,\nreserved for the 2026-09-09 adjudication.\n\n*** THE PANEL'S DOMINANT FINDING IS THAT MY BRIEF POSED THE WRONG QUESTION, AND FOUR SEATS REACHED\nIT INDEPENDENTLY: THE FORK IS MIS-POSED AS ONE DISPOSITION FOR ALL SIX ARMS. ***\n\nThe kimi substitute put it sharpest, and it is a self-inflicted wound: *\"The fork as posed (one\nanswer for the file) repeats the exact error the #447 census inlines a warning against.\"*  **My\nbrief QUOTED `#447`'s own sentence -- \"Saying 'the store is upstream' once for the file would have\nbeen wrong three times out of four.  Check it per site.\" -- and then asked for a single answer for\nsix arms.**  The warning was inlined as a settled premise and violated in the questions below it.\n\n**WHERE THE SEATS AGREE (4 of 5):** the five PURE-STORAGE arms and `LCA_IOC_CONF` must be decided\nseparately, because CONF is the only one with a consumer (`bus = d->ioc_conf & 1`).  For the five,\npremise 6 survives -- grok's formulation is the one to keep: **\"B copies RCR1's *edit* without\nRCR1's *reason*.\"**  RCR1's warrant was that the stored value has a meaning the model otherwise\nhonours; for the five window/TB_ENA fields nothing honours anything, so the edit does not transfer.\nThe kimi substitute sharpens even the agreement: the right analogy for the five is not \"the lie is\npre-existing\" but **`#447`'s ICR cell -- *no honored state to corrupt*** -- which is a stronger and\ndifferently-grounded warrant than the one my premise 6 offered.\n\n**WHERE THEY SPLIT, AND IT IS A REAL DISPUTE TO SETTLE BY MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN BY COUNT:**\n\n    CONF -> B                 glm       \"premise 2 names a live consumer, so CONF is exactly RCR1-shaped\"\n    CONF -> A, more so        minimax   \"the consumer uses only bit 0, so a rejected value leaves it\n                                         correct -- and B can SILENTLY BREAK it if the retained old\n                                         value differs in bit 0\"\n    CONF -> decline or mask   kimi      split per arm\n    CONF -> neither           grok      \"treat it as a bit-mask leftover, not a window\"\n\n**minimax's objection to B is the one with a measurable consequence** and it is the opposite of\nglm's: under B a rejected CONF write leaves the PREVIOUS value in place, and if the previous value\ndiffers in bit 0 the PCI bus selection silently changes -- so B is not merely conservative there, it\ncan alter a consumer that A leaves correct.  That is checkable and should be checked before either\nis adopted.\n\n**THE DISSENT, recorded as a dissent and not as noise:** deepseek alone says premise 6 is unsound\nfor all six -- *\"the guard's accepted-value set is the model's CONTRACT.  Storing a rejected value\nand returning it on readback creates a new false belief that the value is supported, even if\naccepted values are also unimplemented.  The existing lie for accepted values does not license\nexpanding the set of lies.\"*  It also warns the reasoning generalises: it would justify removing any\nstore-before-guard exit anywhere.  **That generalisation objection is the strongest single argument\nagainst A and is not answered by the five-versus-one split**, because it attacks the warrant rather\nthan its scope.\n\n**A BRIEF DEFECT OF MINE, named by glm and correct:** premise 2's field counts were *asserted, not\ninlined* -- I ran the greps and wrote the conclusion instead of the OUTPUT.  This project's own rule\nis that a brief inlines the DERIVER'S OUTPUT rather than a literal, precisely so a packet-fed seat\ncan check it.  glm could not settle five of the six arms from the packet for exactly that reason and\nsaid so honestly rather than ratifying.\n\n**NOT ADOPTED, PENDING codex AND THE ROSTER.**  The next brief is per-arm by construction, carries\nthe deriver output rather than its summary, and puts minimax's silently-broken-consumer prediction\nas a question a measurement can answer.\n\n*** THE grok BODY, READ IN FULL 2026-09-07 -- AND NONE OF THE FOLLOWING WAS IN ITS VERDICT LINE.\nThis is the read-the-body rule paying off on the same day a row was filed about not reading. ***\n\n**1. IT ANSWERS deepseek's DISSENT, which the five/one split could not.**  deepseek's objection was\nthat premise 6 generalises -- it would license removing any store-before-guard exit anywhere.  grok\nscopes it precisely: *\"premise 6 is not the reasoning that lets every store-before-guard site keep\nits store.  It is the reasoning that lets a site keep its store WHEN NO ACCEPTED VALUE OF THAT FIELD\nIS CONSUMED.  That is #447's own per-site rule, not an evasion of it.\"*  The warrant is a per-site\ntest with a checkable predicate, not a blanket.\n\n**2. PREMISE 5 SCORED: \"The comments exist.  The transfer does not.\"**  RCR1 refuses CONSUMED\nFEATURE BITS; these guards whitelist UNIMPLEMENTED CONSTANTS.  That is the distinction the whole\nfork turned on, and it is sharper than my premise 6 offered.\n\n**3. *** OPTION A OPENS A NEW KILL PATH, AND THIS IS THE ROUND'S SAFETY FINDING. ***  Under A, a CONF\nwrite with extra bits that ALSO sets bit 0 reaches `lca_pci_conf`'s `bus != 0` exit(1) -- *\"newly\nreachable ... today that write dies in the CONF guard first.\"*  **A is not purely a reduction; it\nconverts one kill into a different reachable one.**  Any round shipping A must say so, and should\nconsider whether `lca_pci_conf` is in scope with it.\n\n**4. THE FILE'S OWN IDIOM IS A, AND THE GUARDS ARE THE OUTLIERS.**  In the SAME switch:\n`LCA_IOC_BASE` (HAE) and `TBIA` already silently ignore; `W_T_BASE0/1` already accept-and-store; and\nthe `dev >= 21` loop already deletes a kill and substitutes a default.  *\"The value guards are the\noutliers in THIS switch, not the house style.\"*  That is a stronger warrant than anything my brief\noffered, and it is one grep away.\n\n**5. THE ANNOUNCE TEXT WOULD BECOME A WRONG RECORD THE MOMENT A LANDS.**  The current string says\n*\"the only implemented value\"* about a value that **is not implemented**.  The honest once-message is\n*\"window translation / extra CONF bits not implemented\"*, not *\"illegal encoding\"*.  Rewrite it or\ndelete it with the exit -- do not carry it forward.\n\n**6. OPTION-SET CORRECTIONS.**  `#443`'s restore is *\"B wearing extra lines\"* -- observationally B\nfor readback, since nothing re-enters between the store and the `break`; not a distinct member.\n**STAT0 is the architecturally correct error path for LCA** (`IOC_STAT0_ERR` is R/W1C) and is\n**BLOCKED**: STAT0/STAT1 have no case and hit `default:` exit(1) -- *\"You cannot report an error\nthrough a register that currently kills the host.\"*  That member owes a STAT0 implementation first,\nwhich is a different round.  **FAULT confirmed unavailable**: `memory_rw.c` raises on `res <= 0` only\nunder MEM_MIPS and MEM_M88K; Alpha compiles with MEM_ALPHA and has no arm, so `return 0` fails the\naccess with no guest exception.\n\n**7. MASKING CONF IS UNLICENSED, WITH A REASON I DID NOT HAVE: THERE IS NO DATASHEET.**  No 21066\ndocument is in the packet or in the tracked `_scratchpad/*.pdf` list, so RAZ/WI versus store-full is\n**not settleable from this tree**.  Premise 3 stands and masking would be an addition without a\nwarrant.\n\n*** 8. THE WITNESS IS NOT PENDING -- THE RECIPE EXISTS.  This moves the round off documented-only. ***\n* **Machine: `alphabook1` ONLY.**  `machine_alpha.c`'s `device_add(machine, \"lca\")` is under\n  `ST_ALPHABOOK1` and no other subtype.\n* **Addresses** (K0SEG | physical, because `alpha_translate_v2p` identity-maps K0SEG in kernel mode):\n  CONF `0xfffffc0180000020`, TB_ENA `...00a0`, BASE0 `...0100`, BASE1 `...0120`, MASK0 `...0140`,\n  MASK1 `...0160`.\n* **`regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py` ALREADY issues a guest `stq`/`ldq` through this exact decode at\n  CONF on unmodified `alphabook1`**; the other five are further cases of the same DEVICE_ACCESS on\n  the same mapping.\n* **Three traps that file already paid for, so a new probe need not rediscover them:** debugger\n  `dump` skips `DM_DEFAULT` handlers; a debugger `put d` of a killing value IS a host kill but is\n  NOT a guest instruction (so it does not witness); and HAE at window offset 0 silently succeeds, so\n  **a miss control must not use `LCA_IOC_BASE+0`**.\n\n**9. THE KILL CENSUS FOR THE WHOLE FILE:** eleven live `exit(1)` today; **A removes six; five\nremain** -- the `default:` arm (both reads AND writes, covering STAT0/STAT1 and the rest of the\n512 MB IOC window), `lca_pci_conf`'s `bus != 0`, `lca_isa`'s `ofs > len` (filed as [[lcaisalane]]),\nand the bare `lca_interrupt_assert`/`_deassert` TODO pair (member 7, not this round).  Residuals not\nof this class: HAE writes discarded with no storage; TBIA ignored; `default:` still narrows\n`relative_addr` through `(int)` -- **`#506` fixed the `idata` half only**; and [[lcashiftub]].\n\n**10. A CENSUS ERROR IN THIS ROW'S OWN HISTORY:** the guards are TWO predicate shapes, not one --\nbit-masks (CONF, TB_ENA) and equality whitelists (the four windows) -- **and `W_BASE0` accepts TWO\nvalues**, so the row's original \"four registers accept exactly one value each\" framing was false of\n`BASE0` before the title was even corrected to six.  One DESIGN still covers all six (delete the\nexit, keep the store, optional latch); one design does NOT if you pick B, because *\"B is a coherent\nanswer only for a consumed field, which five of these are not.\"*\n\n*** THE codex SEAT LANDED LAST AND OVERTURNED THE PANEL ON A FACT CHECKABLE IN ONE GREP.  MY\nPREMISE 6 IS DEAD, AND SO IS THE FORMULATION I HAD CREDITED grok WITH. ***\n\n**codex's verdict:** *\"Your argument for A fails as written: RCR1 also has no functional consumer.\nI recommend B for pass 1 as a conservative policy choice; hardware-correct write/readback behavior\nremains unproved.\"*\n\n**VERIFIED HERE, not taken on trust** -- `grep -rn rtc_rcr1 src/` returns exactly FOUR occurrences:\n\n    dev_sh4.c:388    uint8_t   rtc_rcr1;              declaration\n    dev_sh4.c:2776   odata = d->rtc_rcr1;             READBACK\n    dev_sh4.c:2781   ...landed in rtc_rcr1 and...     INSIDE THE COMMENT\n    dev_sh4.c:2805   d->rtc_rcr1 = idata;             STORE\n\n**Zero functional consumers.**  And the comparison that settles it: `ioc_conf` also has four\noccurrences, but its fourth is `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` -- a real consumer.  **So RCR1 is consumed\nLESS than LCA's CONF, not more.**\n\n*** WHAT THIS DESTROYS. ***  My premise 6 claimed LCA differs from RCR1 because *\"RCR1's stored value\nhas a meaning the model otherwise honours\"*.  It has no such meaning: the model stores it and reads\nit back, exactly like the five LCA window fields.  **grok's formulation, which I recorded above as\n\"the keeper\" -- *\"RCR1 refuses CONSUMED FEATURE BITS; these guards whitelist UNIMPLEMENTED\nCONSTANTS\"* -- is FALSE IN ITS FIRST HALF.**  So is my own gloss that `#447`'s edit \"does not\ntransfer\".\n\n**IT TRANSFERS EXACTLY.**  `#447` faced a store-before-guard field with NO functional consumer,\nand chose to MOVE THE STORE BELOW THE GUARD.  That is the LCA shape, arm for arm.  The tree's own\nprecedent is therefore evidence FOR B, which is what codex, deepseek and (for CONF) glm all said.\n\n*** AND THE SURVIVING ARGUMENT IS THE ONE deepseek RAISED FIRST AND codex SHARPENED: THE GUEST IS\nITSELF A CONSUMER OF READBACK. ***  codex: *\"Previously, a rejected write ended execution before\nsubsequent guest code could observe that value.  A creates that observation and allows the guest to\nmake further decisions from it.  Existing incomplete behavior for accepted settings does not\nestablish that these newly admitted executions are equally harmless.\"*  That argument is untouched by\nthe consumer census, because it does not depend on a MODEL consumer at all -- and it is precisely\nwhat `#447`'s comment means by *\"the guest writes CIE|AIE, reads CIE|AIE back, and believes the RTC\nwill interrupt it.\"*\n\n**TALLY, and the argument decides it rather than the count:** codex -> B; deepseek -> B; glm -> B for\nCONF and not settleable for the five; kimi substitute -> split per arm; grok -> A; minimax -> A.  But\ngrok's and minimax's A both rest on the consumed/unconsumed distinction, **and that distinction is\nnow measured false.**  Count the arguments, not the seats: the load-bearing premise under the A\nposition has been refuted by a grep.\n\n**THIS IS THE THIRD REVERSAL IN THIS ROUND'S DESIGN AND IT COST NOTHING, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THE\nHOLD DISCIPLINE.**  No code is committed; the round has produced adjudication text and zero product\nchanges.  The earlier records above -- premise 6 \"sound for five arms\", grok's formulation as \"the\nkeeper\" -- are left standing WITH this correction beneath them rather than silently edited, because\na future reader needs to see that the panel's majority reasoning was overturned and by what.\n\n*** WHAT IS NOW OWED BEFORE ANY FIX: the ARM-BY-ARM design, with the RCR1 precedent read as\nSUPPORTING B, and codex's readback-consumer argument answered rather than assumed away.  The\nquestion that remains genuinely open is minimax's measurable one -- under B a rejected CONF write\nretains the previous value, and codex adds that W_MASK0/W_BASE1/W_MASK1 RESET TO ZERO, which is NOT\nin their accepted set, so \"retain the last accepted value\" is not even well-defined at reset.  That\nis a concrete defect in B as stated and it needs an answer (retain? force the reset value? decline\nonly the guarded bits?) before B can ship. ***\n\n*** AND THE SHARPEST LESSON IS NOT THE REVERSAL, IT IS HOW MY GLOSS BEAT MY OWN PRIMARY SOURCE. ***\n\nThe brief inlined #447s RCR1 comment VERBATIM.  That comment argues purely from READBACK:\n\n    the guest writes CIE|AIE, reads CIE|AIE back, and believes the RTC will\n    interrupt it.  It never will.\n\n**It never claims a model consumer.**  The consumed/unconsumed framing is MINE -- premise 6 glossed\nit as RCR1s stored value having a meaning the model otherwise honours -- and grok adopted my\ngloss rather than the quoted text sitting directly above it, producing B copies RCR1s edit\nwithout RCR1s reason.  codex went to the source, grepped the field, and found the gloss false.\n\n> **INLINING THE PRIMARY SOURCE DOES NOT PROTECT A BRIEF FROM A WRONG SUMMARY PLACED BESIDE IT.**\n> A seat reads the summary as the QUESTION and the quote as SUPPORTING MATERIAL, so a false gloss\n> steers the answer even when the refuting text is on the same page.  This projects premise-audit\n> rule says to state load-bearing premises AS QUESTIONS; the refinement is that a premise which\n> RESTATES an inlined quotation is the most dangerous kind, because it looks already verified.\n\n*The cheap defence is the one codex used and I did not: when a premise characterises what some\nother code does, GREP THAT CODE.  `grep -rn rtc_rcr1 src/` is four lines of output and it was\navailable to me before the brief went out.*\n\n*** codex, `#511` PASS 2, CHALLENGES THE \"GRACEFUL DEGRADATION PRECEDENT\" THAT BOTH LCA ROWS LEAN\nON. ***\n\n*\"`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`'s fallback selects device zero and forwards the access through\n`bus_pci_setaddr`.  **That is an ALIASING DECISION, not by itself proof of harmless degradation.**\nThe comment's 'transferable warrant' needs its own justification; the shift repair does not establish\nit.\"*\n\nThe `dev >= 21` arm -- commented-out `fatal()`/`exit(1)` with `dev = 0;` substituted -- has been\ncited repeatedly here, including by [[lcabadval]], as the file's own in-tree warrant for deleting a\nhost kill.  **codex's point is that substituting device ZERO is not a decline: it silently redirects\nthe access to a DIFFERENT DEVICE.**  Whether that is harmless is a separate question nobody has\nasked, and \"someone already did it in this file\" is not the same as \"it is right\".\n\n> **A TRANSFERRED WARRANT NEEDS ITS OWN JUSTIFICATION, NOT JUST A PRECEDENT.**  This is the\n> option-set rule's own caution turned around: grepping the fork for an existing idiom finds what the\n> tree DOES, which is evidence about practice and not about correctness.\n\n*Not resolved here.  Recorded on both rows because both cite it, and any round that leans on it owes\nthe justification codex is asking for.*\n\n*** THE THIRD OPTION-SET MEMBER IS NAMED, AND THE TWO-OPTION FRAMING WAS REALLY A ONE-OPTION\nFRAMING.  codex, 2026-09-08; the two load-bearing claims VERIFIED here before recording. ***\n\n**VERDICT: C -- decline the out-of-set write, PRESERVE the previous register value, announce once.**\n\n### 1.  A AND B OVERLAP.  There was never a choice between them.\n\n*\"Your reconstructed A and B overlap: both store the written value and continue.  With the existing\nreads retained, B describes SHADOW STORAGE WITH READBACK, not a write-only register.  It supplies no\ndistinct state-transition policy.\"*  **So the panel that argued A-versus-B was arguing about one\noption under two names** -- the option-set-completeness failure in its purest form, and this row had\nit for two rounds.\n\n### 2.  *** WHAT `#447` ACTUALLY DOES IS DECLINE-AND-PRESERVE, AND I HAD IT BACKWARDS. ***\n\nThe precedent was cited as \"a register with no consumer, where the write is accepted\".  The\nimplementation says otherwise -- **the store is in the ELSE branch**:\n\n    if (idata & (SH_RCR1_CIE | SH_RCR1_AIE)) {\n            /* bounded diagnostic */\n    } else\n            d->rtc_rcr1 = idata;\n\n*\"Unsupported writes preserve the previous value.  They are NOT accepted into backing storage.\"*  And\nthe reason recorded in that comment is a READBACK concern -- echoing interrupt-enable flags would\nsuggest functionality the model does not provide -- so **absence of a consumer neither disqualifies\ndecline-and-preserve nor argues for accepting everything**: readback is guest-observable on its own.\n\n### 3.  *** `ioc_conf` HAS A FUNCTIONAL CONSUMER.  VERIFIED. ***\n\n`dev_lca.c:121` is `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1;`, feeding the PCI decode.  **The row's \"no functional\nconsumer\" premise is false for CONF**, whatever it is for the other five.\n\n> **AND IT TIES TWO HIGH ROWS TOGETHER.**  `lcaconfbus1kill` is precisely about that consumer: a\n> write of `0x1` makes `bus == 1` and arms the `bus != 0` host kill.  `0x1` is INSIDE this arm's\n> accepted set `{0, 1}`, so **C does not protect against it and must not be described as if it\n> does.**  The two rows are separate defects on one register and both need fixing.\n\n### 4.  THE RESET QUESTION IS DISSOLVED, NOT ANSWERED -- and the reason improves the fix.\n\n*\"The predicates govern WRITES, not all possible stored states.\"*  Initialization assigns zero with\nno guard; reads return the stored value with no guard; **and the write assigns BEFORE the guard is\nevaluated.**  So the model can return zero at reset while refusing a later write of zero:\n*\"that asymmetry is established; it is not a logical contradiction.\"*\n\n    register  writes the guard accepts        reset 0 passes?\n    CONF      {0, 1}                          yes\n    TB_ENA    {0, 0x80}                       yes\n    W_BASE0   {0, 0x300800000}                yes\n    W_MASK0   {0x700000}                      NO\n    W_BASE1   {0x240000000}                   NO\n    W_MASK1   {0x3ff00000}                    NO\n\n*** MY OWN ADDITION, from verifying the store-order claim at `:233` vs the guard at `:236`: BECAUSE\nTHE STORE HAPPENS FIRST, SIMPLY DELETING THE `exit(1)` LEAVES THE BAD VALUE STORED. ***  That is not\na weaker version of C -- it is a DIFFERENT OBSERVABLE STATE, readable by the guest through the\nexisting read arm.  **C requires moving each store behind its guard, exactly as RCR1 does.**\n\n### 5.  THE SIBLING EVIDENCE SUPPORTS BOTH POLICIES AND SELECTS NEITHER\n\nInside this same switch:\n\n    LCA_IOC_BASE, LCA_IOC_TBIA      discard writes; reads return 0; success\n    LCA_IOC_W_T_BASE0/1             store writes and return them on reads\n    default:                        diagnose and TERMINATE, no backing storage\n\n*\"Thus the sibling evidence supports BOTH discarding writes and retaining shadow state.  It does not\nselect a universal policy.\"*  **A useful negative result: the grep-the-fork rule found the member but\ncannot settle the policy here**, because the file is internally inconsistent.\n\n*`default:` is explicitly OUT of this round -- it has no previous value to preserve, so C is not even\nexpressible there.*\n\n### 6.  THE CENSUS SURVIVES ITS AUDIT\n\nEleven live explicit `exit(1)`: the six value arms at **248, 268, 283, 298, 322, 337**, plus\n**73, 85, 167, 196, 357**.  A bare `exit(` grep returns thirteen; two are comments (the explanatory\nmention and the disabled call whose live fallback is `dev = 0`).  *This is the row's third census and\nthe first to survive.\n\n*** CORRECTION, WITHIN THE HOUR AND BEFORE THIS PROPAGATED: \"THE THIRD OPTION-SET MEMBER\" IS WRONG.\nTHERE IS NO THIRD MEMBER.  THE OPTION SET WAS ALREADY RIGHT AND **MY BRIEF CORRUPTED IT**. ***\n\n`tools/pipeline/fable_queue.md` §7i states the fork exactly as the earlier panel left it:\n\n    A  delete fatal()+exit(1) from all six arms, KEEP the store that already ran\n    B  DECLINE -- move the store BELOW the guard, as #447 did for SH4's RCR1\n\n**The queue's B IS codex's C.**  Decline-and-preserve was already ON the table, correctly described,\nwith the `#447` precedent already attached to it.  My `DISPOSITION2_brief.md` reconstructed B as\n*\"treat the register as write-only backing state\"* -- which is NOT what B says -- and that\nreconstruction is what made A and B overlap.\n\n> **SO THE OPTION-SET FAILURE WAS IN THE BRIEF, NOT IN THE ROW.**  The row had two genuinely\n> distinct members; the brief collapsed them into one and then asked the panel for a third.  *A\n> brief that mis-states the options gets a panel arguing about the wrong set* -- which is the\n> option-set rule's own warning, arriving from the direction it does not usually name: not a MISSING\n> member, an ALTERED one.\n\n**codex flagged this itself and I read past it:** *\"If the earlier panel called decline-and-preserve\n'B,' its letter assignment differs from your reconstruction.  The quoted verdict alone cannot\nresolve that historical naming.  Its implemented precedent is unambiguous.\"*  **It hedged the exact\nerror, derived the right disposition anyway from the `#447` source, and I recorded its answer under a\nletter that does not exist in this row's own queue entry.**\n\n*** WHAT SURVIVES THE CORRECTION, and it is nearly all of it: ***\n\n* **The DISPOSITION is unchanged and now has two independent derivations** -- the earlier panel\n  reached decline-and-preserve as B, and codex reached it from `#447`'s implementation without\n  trusting my framing.\n* **The `#447` reading is new and load-bearing**: the store sits in the ELSE branch, so unsupported\n  writes PRESERVE the previous value.  *The brief had that backwards.*\n* **`ioc_conf`'s consumer at `dev_lca.c:121` is new**, and it ties this row to `lcaconfbus1kill`.\n* **The store-before-guard order at `:233`/`:236` is new**, and it is what makes A and B differ in\n  OBSERVABLE STATE rather than only in tidiness.\n* **The reset-coherence dissolution and the accepted-set table are new.**\n* **The census survived a third audit.**\n\n**What does NOT survive is the sentence \"the third option-set member is named\".**  Nothing was\nmissing.  *Recorded in place rather than deleted, because a correction that hides what it corrects\nis how this row got to three reversals in the first place.*\n\n*** A FACT THIS ROW'S QUESTION DOES NOT CARRY, ESTABLISHED 2026-09-08 FROM THREE INDEPENDENT FILES:\nOPTION-SET MEMBER 5 (FAULT) IS NOT AVAILABLE ON ALPHA. ***\n\nThis is an Alpha machine, and the adjudication of this row weighs dispositions from the standing\noption set.  **FAULT looks available and is not**, which is the option-set-completeness problem\ninverted -- not a missing member, a PHANTOM one.\n\n    memory_rw.c        the device-failure dispatcher has MIPS and M88K exception arms and\n                       NO ALPHA ARM                          (found via dev_lca.c, `lcaisalane`)\n    memory_alpha.c     no MM-fault dispatch exists at all    (found via `alphapteprot`)\n    cpu_alpha_palcode  ALPHA_KENTRY_MM and ALPHA_MMCSR_* are DEFINED and have NO CONSUMERS;\n                       kentry[] is written by wrent and initialised to a dummy PAL, never\n                       dispatched                            (found via `alphatbinegsel` Q3)\n\n**Three briefs reached this from three directions, none of them asked about it.**  And a fourth\nseat priced it: choosing FAULT obliges building delivery first -- a PAL frame,\n`kentry[ALPHA_KENTRY_MM]`, MMCSR/VA, and teaching `pc_to_pointers` about a redirected PC.  *\"That is\na new IMPLEMENT round, not a de-fatalisation.\"*\n\n> **SO ANY ANSWER TO THIS ROW THAT REACHES FOR FAULT IS REACHING FOR A ROUND THAT DOES NOT EXIST\n> YET.**  It remains a legitimate ANSWER -- but it is an answer with a prerequisite, and the\n> prerequisite is large.  Recorded here so the adjudication prices it rather than assuming it.\n\n*** THE NOTE ABOVE UNDERSTATED THE SCOPE, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART: FAULT IS NOT\n\"UNAVAILABLE ON ALPHA\".  IT IS AVAILABLE ON **MIPS AND M88K ONLY**, FOR EVERY ARCHITECTURE IN THIS\nTREE.  Derived 2026-09-08 by opening the dispatcher. ***\n\n`src/cpus/memory_rw.c`, the device-failure path -- the whole of it:\n\n    if (res <= 0 && !no_exceptions) {\n            debug(\"[ %s device '%s' addr %08lx failed ]\");\n    #ifdef MEM_MIPS\n            mips_cpu_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION? EXCEPTION_IBE : EXCEPTION_DBE, ...);\n    #endif\n    #ifdef MEM_M88K\n            m88k_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION ? M88K_EXCEPTION_INSTRUCTION_ACCESS\n                                                           : M88K_EXCEPTION_DATA_ACCESS, 0);\n    #endif\n            return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED;\n    }\n\n**TWO `#ifdef` ARMS.  There is no ARM arm, no Alpha arm, no PPC arm and no SH arm** -- every other\narchitecture reaches `return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` with no exception delivered at all.\n\n> **SO OPTION-SET MEMBER 5 IS A PHANTOM ON EVERY ARCHITECTURE BUT TWO**, and a round on an ARM, SH,\n> PPC or Alpha device that reaches for FAULT is reaching for a round that does not exist yet.\n\n*Found because I wrote the OPPOSITE into a brief.  Assessing `fbfiqsilent` -- an ARM footbridge row\n-- I told the seat that \"member 5 FAULT may genuinely be available here, `memory_rw`'s\ndevice-failure path has an ARM arm.\"  It replied that the claimed arm **is absent from this tree**,\nand opening the file confirms it.  **I was one brief away from spreading a false availability claim\nacross the ARM queue**, having just corrected three rows for the Alpha version of it.*\n\n*** THE 21066 MANUAL IS LOCAL NOW, AND IT PUTS A NUMBER ON THIS ROW FOR THE FIRST TIME -- BUT THE\nNUMBER IS MY DERIVATION AND IS OUT WITH A PANEL, NOT ESTABLISHED. ***\n\n**QUOTED, `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt:9572-9578`, read locally:**\n\n> *\"The window mask register IOC_W_MASK provides a mask corresponding to bits <31:20> of an\n> incoming PCI address.  The size of each window is programmed to be from 1 MB to 4 GB ...  Table\n> 6-6 shows the supported values of IOC_W_MASK register bits <31:20>; **other values are\n> unsupported**.\"*\n\n**Table 6-6 (`:9581-9598`) has THIRTEEN rows** -- a doubling run from 1 MB (`0x000`) to 4 GB\n(`0xfff`), each a contiguous run of low ones.\n\n**MY DERIVATION, flagged as mine:** the register value is the field shifted left 20, so the fork's\ntwo accepted constants are\n\n    LCA_IOC_W_MASK0   accepts ONLY 0x700000     ->  field 0x007  ->    8 MB   legal, 1 of 13\n    LCA_IOC_W_MASK1   accepts ONLY 0x3ff00000   ->  field 0x3ff  ->    1 GB   legal, 1 of 13\n\nand the legal set is exactly `(f & (f + 1)) == 0`, verified over all thirteen.\n\n> **IF THAT IS RIGHT, the model terminates the host on TWELVE DOCUMENTED-LEGAL VALUES PER MASK\n> REGISTER -- 24 across the two -- and a guest programming any window size other than 8 MB / 1 GB\n> is doing something the hardware manual explicitly supports.**  That is a much sharper severity\n> statement than *\"rejects values outside a small accepted set\"*, because it names the denominator.\n\n*** IT IS NOT RECORDED AS ESTABLISHED, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. ***  The load-bearing step is my own\narithmetic, done in one sitting, on a table extracted by `pdftotext` -- and this project's rule is\nthat an extracted table can stagger names against values.  **Table 6-6 LOOKS self-calibrating (a\ndoubling bit-run beside a doubling size run, thirteen against thirteen), but the calibration rule\nsays one point is only valid for a UNIFORM stagger and you cannot tell which kind you have from one\npoint.**  So it went out to a panel with *\"check the premises, not just the derivations\"* and with\nthe derivation shown step by step:\n\n    regress/artifacts/lcabadval/MANUAL_brief.md   file-reading seats: codex, grok -- also asked to\n                                                  read S6.4 for the OTHER FOUR arms and to give a\n                                                  count WITH ITS DENOMINATOR per register\n    regress/artifacts/lcabadval/PACKET_brief.md   packet-fed seats: glm, deepseek, minimax, kimi --\n                                                  Table 6-6 inlined verbatim so they can check the\n                                                  arithmetic without file access\n\n**WHAT IS STILL OPEN, and the brief asks for each explicitly:** the manual enumerates legal values\nfor W_MASK and (as far as I read) NOT for W_BASE, so the two registers may need different\ndispositions; `0x300800000` and `0x240000000` are 34-bit values and I have not established how wide\nthese registers are or what bits above 31 mean; and the more dangerous error direction is a real\nquestion -- terminating on a legal value, versus accepting a value the model's own address decode\nthen does not honour.\n\n*** THE PANEL ANSWERED: SIX SEATS, MY COUNT CONFIRMED -- AND THE ONE SEAT THAT DISSENTED MADE THE\nDEFECT BIGGER, NOT SMALLER. ***\n\n    codex     CONFIRMS 12/13 per register, 24/26 register-value pairs, and Table 6-6 IS\n              self-calibrating -- it parsed all thirteen rows and checked the adjacent runs\n    glm       \"the S3 arithmetic is right -- I re-derived every step and found no defect\"\n    kimi      \"your derivation is right\": shift, both fields, the count, the invariant\n    minimax   \"the S3 derivation is correct\"\n    grok      answered, 14,818 b\n    deepseek  CORRECT ONLY IF bits <19:0> must be zero -- AND THAT PREMISE IS NOT ESTABLISHED\n              BY THE TEXT I QUOTED\n\n*** deepseek IS RIGHT, AND codex SETTLED IT FROM THE OTHER END. ***  My quote enumerates bits\n<31:20> and says nothing about <19:0>.  codex read the preface: **RES fields are type `X` --\nIGNORED ON WRITES, unpredictable on reads, NOT must-be-zero** (`21066_66a_hrm.txt:955`, `:985`).\n\n> **SO THE GUARD IS WORSE THAN \"ACCEPTS ONE OF THIRTEEN\".  IT TESTS THE WHOLE 64-BIT OPERAND\n> AGAINST A SINGLE LITERAL -- `if (idata != 0x700000ULL)` -- WHILE THE HARDWARE DEFINES A 12-BIT\n> FIELD AND IGNORES THE REST.**  A guest writing `0x7000ff`, functionally identical on real\n> hardware, terminates the emulator.  *deepseek said so in as many words: \"if lower bits are\n> ignored, the number of legal register values is much larger than 13 per mask, and the code is\n> even more restrictive.\"*\n\n**AND IT SUPPLIED THE TRAP A NAIVE FIX WOULD HIT:** the invariant must be applied to the SHIFTED\nFIELD.  `(idata & (idata + 1)) == 0` on the full value **REJECTS THE LEGAL `0x700000`**, because\n`0x700000 & 0x700001 == 0x700000`.  The guard is `f = idata >> 20; (f & (f + 1)) == 0`.\n\n*** FOUR MORE CORRECTIONS FROM codex, EACH NARROWING OR SHARPENING A CLAIM OF MINE. ***\n\n* **MY OWN TABLE DROPPED A ROW.**  `MANUAL_brief.md` reformatted Table 6-6 into three columns and\n  **lost `0000 0001 1111` = 32 MB** -- twelve rows under a sentence saying thirteen.  The PACKET\n  brief has all thirteen.  *A transcription error in the brief that asks others to check my\n  transcription.*\n* **ALIGNMENT AND NON-OVERLAP, which my quote stopped one paragraph short of** (`:9618`, S6.4.9):\n  bases must align naturally to window size and windows must not overlap.  **With the two accepted\n  bases held FIXED, window 0 admits only the four sizes through 8 MB and window 1 the eleven\n  through 1 GB** -- so in that configuration the guards reject 3/4 and 10/11.  All thirteen sizes\n  are supported; changing size can require changing the surrounding configuration.\n* **`IOC_CONF` IS A TWO-BIT FIELD, values 0..3, so `idata & ~1` rejects 2 of 4** defined settings --\n  I had quoted the guard without checking the field width.\n* **`IOC_TB_ENA` IS NOT A FUNCTIONAL DEFECT AT ALL: TEN has exactly two states and BOTH are\n  accepted -- 0/2.**  Its only defect is the ignored-bit one.  *Recorded because a round that\n  \"fixes six arms\" would be overclaiming.*\n\n*** THE SHARPEST SINGLE FINDING, AND IT IS NOT MINE: THE MANUAL REQUIRES INITIALISING BOTH BASE\nREGISTERS TO ZERO (S6.4.14, `:10788`), AND `LCA_IOC_W_BASE1` REJECTS EXACTLY THAT. ***  A guest\nfollowing the manual own documented initialisation sequence terminates the emulator.  That is not a\ncorner value -- it is the prescribed setup.\n\n**Also established:** the registers are **64-bit, quadword access only** (S6.4 excludes longword,\n`:10175`); and **all six arms STORE THE VALUE BEFORE TESTING IT** (`d->window_mask_0 = idata;`\nprecedes the guard), so device state is already updated when the process dies.\n\n**DISPOSITION IS NOT SETTLED HERE.**  deepseek proposes accept-with-backing-storage for the values\nthe manual or existing code already treats as legal, plus a `(once)` decline otherwise, and warns\nagainst expanding the W_BASE set without evidence since the manual enumerates none.  That is a\ndesign question for the round, and the round must not silently widen an accept set.\n\n*** THE FIX DESIGN IS IN, AND IT QUALIFIES THREE OF THE ASSESS PANEL'S FINDINGS -- ONE OF THEM A\nCONCLUSION I HAD ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN. ***\n\n**VERDICT (codex):** *preserve masked register state; validate `W_MASK` FIELDS; remove the\n`W_BASE` magic-value whitelists; leave TEN's two-state policy alone but correct `TB_ENA`'s\nreserved-bit guard.*  **\"This fixes register handling, not DMA translation\"** -- the scope\nsentence the round must carry.\n\n* *** `TB_ENA` IS NOT EXEMPT AFTER ALL, AND MY RECORD SAID IT WAS. ***  I recorded *\"IOC_TB_ENA is\n  NOT a functional defect at all -- TEN has exactly two states and both are accepted (0/2)\"*.  That\n  is right **only for normalised TEN values**: the arm's other bits are `RES`, type `X`, so the\n  existing whole-operand guard **still rejects writes the hardware ignores**.  Leaving the arm\n  untouched would contradict the very answer given to Q2.  *A per-field finding restated as a\n  per-arm exemption -- the dropped-qualifier class again.*\n* **ALL SIX REGISTERS ARE WRITE-ONLY, not RW.**  The manual makes reads of IOC write-only registers\n  UNPREDICTABLE, so **preserving readback is a useful existing emulator convention, not a hardware\n  requirement.**  A round that \"accepts with backing storage\" should say which of the two it is\n  doing.\n* **AND A PRECISION CORRECTION TO HOW I STATED THE RES RULE:** *\"`RES` alone does not mean ignored\n  -- the decisive evidence is each register table's type `X`, interpreted through the\n  conventions.\"*  My record leaned on \"RES fields are type X\" as one fact; it is two, and only the\n  second carries the ignored-on-write conclusion.\n* **`IOC_CONF` has four REPRESENTABLE field encodings**, and calling them four *supported PCI\n  operating modes* goes beyond the evidence -- the HRM also defers to the PCI specification's\n  configuration-cycle encoding restrictions.  So the \"2 of 4 rejected\" figure is a count of\n  representable encodings, not of legal modes.\n\n**Table 6-6's thirteen entries and the rest of the numerical premises are re-confirmed.**\n\n*** THE SHARP FINDING IS NOT THE OFFSET COUNT.  IT IS THAT TWO DOCUMENTED REGISTERS HAVE NO CASE,\nAND ONE OF THEM IS THE ERROR-STATUS REGISTER A DRIVER READS AFTER A FAILED PROBE.  2026-09-08. ***\n\nA codex ANALYSIS job (the construction-shaped brief is refused; see [[codexcyberflag]]) enumerated\nthe `lca_ioc' region and its stream ended before a verdict.  Its numbers were then VERIFIED here\nrather than taken:\n\n    memory_device_register(..., \"lca_ioc\", LCA_IOC_BASE, 0x20000000, ...)   512 MiB\n    switch (relative_addr + LCA_IOC_BASE)                    NO shift, NO mask before the switch\n    named cases: 10        0x20000000 - 10 = 536,870,902 byte offsets reach the shared `default:'\n\n**That count is arithmetically right and it is the WEAKER half of the finding.**  Most of those\noffsets are not registers at all, and the carrier's own rule says a domain is itself a premise: the\nAlpha alignment gate has not been analysed here, so the count of REACHABLE ACCESSES is smaller than\n536 million by an unmeasured factor.  It is recorded with its deriver and NOT as a severity claim.\n\n*** THE STRONGER HALF, from comparing the HEADER's register list against the switch's cases: ***\n\n    LCA_IOC_HAE        +0x000   HANDLED  (via `case LCA_IOC_BASE:' -- HAE and BASE are the same\n                                          address; see the correction below)\n    LCA_IOC_CONF       +0x020   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_STAT0      +0x040   *** NO CASE ***   Status 0\n    LCA_IOC_STAT1      +0x060   *** NO CASE ***   Status 1\n    LCA_IOC_TBIA       +0x080   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_TB_ENA     +0x0a0   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_W_BASE0/1  +0x100/0x120   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_W_MASK0/1  +0x140/0x160   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_W_T_BASE0/1 +0x180/0x1a0  HANDLED\n\n**TWO of the twelve documented registers have no case, and they are STAT0 and STAT1.**\n\n> *** AND STAT0 IS EXACTLY THE REGISTER THE 21066 MANUAL SAYS RECORDS A FAILED PCI ACCESS.\n> `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt' S6.2.18 (PCI Master Abort): an unanswered read \"returns arbitrary\n> data with error status to the CPU, which causes an instruction exception\", plus an internal\n> interrupt and ERR/CODE/CMD/ADDR logging -- and that log is STAT0. ***\n>\n> **So the ordinary driver error-recovery sequence -- probe a PCI slot, take a master abort, then\n> read the status register to find out what happened -- reaches `fatal()' + `exit(1)'.**  That is\n> not an exotic value or an unusual address.  It is what a driver does when a probe fails.\n\n*** AND IT PUTS THIS ROW ON THE SAME GUEST CODE PATH AS `#515' / [[lcaconfbus1kill]], WHICH MATTERS\nFOR ORDERING RATHER THAN FOR SCOPE. ***  `#515` makes an absent-device config access return the\nempty-slot signature instead of terminating the host.  A driver that then consults STAT0 to check\nfor the error the manual says accompanies it lands here.  **The two rows are adjacent steps of one\ndriver sequence** -- which is an argument for scheduling this row next, and NOT an argument for\nfolding it into `#515` (different handler, different semantic class, different detector).\n\n*** A CORRECTION TO MY OWN CHECK, made before it could mislead: THE FIRST RUN REPORTED **THREE**\nUNHANDLED REGISTERS AND NAMED `LCA_IOC_HAE' AMONG THEM.  THAT IS WRONG. ***  `alpha_lcareg.h`\ndefines `LCA_IOC_HAE` as `LCA_IOC_BASE` -- the same address -- and the switch carries\n`case LCA_IOC_BASE:`, so HAE is handled under the other name.  **My script matched cases by NAME\nwhen the thing that decides handledness is the VALUE.**\n\n> That is this file's own deriver rule landing on me: *\"BEFORE WRITING A DERIVER, ASK WHAT MAKES A\n> MEMBER A MEMBER.  If the answer is a property the regex does not test, the deriver is measuring a\n> CORRELATE, and a correlate's error is invisible because the output is still a plausible number.\"*\n> A by-name census of a switch whose labels are aliases returns a plausible, wrong answer -- and it\n> inflated the finding by 50% in the direction that would have made the row look worse.\n\n**STILL OPEN and deliberately NOT answered here:** whether the two `default:` arms (read and write)\nare symmetric, what the write side does with `idata`, and what the manual says STAT0's reset value\nand semantics are.  The codex job was cut off before Q3-Q5 and its remaining questions stand.\n\n*** THE codex RE-FIRE LANDED WITH A VERDICT, AND IT REFUTED ONE OF MY OWN PREMISES. 2026-09-08. ***\n(The first firing could not resolve the manual -- a repo-relative path, when `_scratchpad/` sits one\nlevel ABOVE the git root -- and said so plainly instead of guessing.  A brief defect, not a seat\nfailure.  Re-fired with the absolute path.)\n\n**MY Q4 ASKED whether the READ and WRITE `default:` arms are symmetric.  codex: the question has no\nsubject.**\n\n    VERDICT: \"The claimed default-arm asymmetry is false: `lca_ioc' has ONE SHARED terminating\n             `default:'.\"\n\nCHECKED: `awk '/DEVICE_ACCESS\\(lca_ioc\\)/,/^}/' | grep -c 'default:'` = **1**.  I had assumed two\narms because the sibling handler `lca_pci_conf` splits by `writeflag`; this one does not.  *A brief\nthat asks about an asymmetry invites an answer about which side is broader, and the honest answer\nwas that there is one side.*\n\n*** THE COUNT, WITH THE ALIGNMENT QUALIFICATION I HAD NOT ASKED FOR AND WHICH THE CARRIER'S OWN\nDOMAIN RULE DEMANDS. ***\n\n    registration     0x180000000, length 0x20000000 = 536,870,912 bytes, ending 0x19fffffff\n    recognised       10 relative offsets: 0x000 (BASE, also HAE), 0x020, 0x080, 0x0a0,\n                     0x100, 0x120, 0x140, 0x160, 0x180, 0x1a0\n    reach default    536,870,902 byte offsets      (= 0x20000000 - 10)\n    QUADWORD-ALIGNED  67,108,854                   <- the honest figure for ordinary accesses\n\n**codex volunteered the alignment split rather than letting the big number stand**, noting the\nhandler adds the base *without shifting, masking, or checking alignment*, and that the permitted\nfile set could not establish what the dispatcher actually delivers.  It also cites the manual (S6.4)\nrequiring quadword IOC accesses -- so the 67 million figure is the one an ordinary driver can reach,\nand unaligned delivery is not needed to make the point.\n\n*** TEN ACTIVE `exit(1)` CALLS IN THIS ONE FILE, enumerated by codex and consistent with what this\nsession has separately measured: ***\n\n    2   the interrupt callbacks -- UNCONDITIONAL on entry, no predicate at all   -> [[lcairqstubkill]]\n    1   the `lca_isa' guard `ofs > len'\n    6   `lca_ioc' value guards\n    1   the shared `lca_ioc' default\n    ---\n    (plus the one `#515' / [[lcaconfbus1kill]] has just REMOVED from `lca_pci_conf')\n\n*** THE `lca_isa' GUARD IS REACHABLE, BUT ONLY BY A BYTE OR WORD ACCESS -- and codex's phrasing was\ncorrectly qualified where a careless reading of it would not be. ***  It said the manual's\n*\"ordinary longword and quadword accesses cannot satisfy `ofs > len'\"*.  Enumerated here rather than\ninferred, since `ofs = relative_addr & 3` is 0..3:\n\n    len >= 4 (longword, quadword)   ofs > len is UNSATISFIABLE          -- codex is right\n    len == 1 (byte)                 ofs = 2 or 3  ->  REACHABLE\n    len == 2 (word)                 ofs = 3       ->  REACHABLE\n\n**So it is not a dead guard; it is a guard no CONFORMING access reaches.**  Three (ofs, len) pairs\nreach it, all of them sub-longword.  *Worth stating precisely because \"the manual says accesses are\nquadword\" is an argument about what a good driver does, not about what the model will accept -- and\nthe model accepts a byte access and then terminates the host.*\n\n**SCOPE, so this row does not silently swallow its neighbours:** the interrupt callbacks are\n[[lcairqstubkill]] and are separately witnessed; the `lca_isa` guard is a DIFFERENT handler with a\ndifferent reachability story and should be its own row rather than folded in here.  This row remains\nthe six `lca_ioc` value guards plus the shared default."
34847:   "state_note": "Surfaced by the pcc2widthkills pass-1 panel (fable5 and kimi independently) while auditing the width fix: 'ten kills removed' would read as the file being safe while a one-byte guest write still exits.  The width detector's GENCTL lanes were chosen (0xd9/0x40, C040 clear) precisely to stay off this kill; c040ok() asserts it at authoring time.  Next round after pcc2widthkills closes.  Filed in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md (dated tail, `### pcc2c040kill`, line 14553 as of 2026-09-04); this row added 2026-09-04 so the dashboard shows it -- the bug file had it, the ledger did not.  NEW FACT 2026-09-05 (Opus design seat on pcc2defaultkill, verified: dev_pcc2.c SCCRXIACK arm has no writeflag test): a guest LOAD of SCCRXIACK 0x27 runs reassert_interrupts(), and OpenBSD's console driver cl.c reads that offset on SCC receive interrupts -- so the ENTRY into the C040 kill is routine guest traffic; only the ARMING (C040 set in GENCTL) is rare.  Reachability better than the filing says.  Must be fixed before any round implements a PCC2 IACK arm.\n\n*** PASS-1 AUDIT 2026-09-07 (codex, `regress/artifacts/pcc2c040kill/codex_pass1_20260907.txt`).\nTHE CENTRAL PREMISE WAS WRONG AND THE CORRECTION MAKES THE DEFECT SIMPLER AND WORSE. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE for unsupported C040 delivery.  Survival is a nonvacuous\noracle here, but cannot establish interrupt correctness.\"*\n\n*** THE CORRECTION: *\"the GENCTL write itself immediately calls the terminating helper.  Arming and\ntermination occur within that access; subsequent SCC traffic is unnecessary.\"* ***\n\n**Line 459 IS `reassert_interrupts()` inside the GENCTL byte-write arm.**  So the whole\n`SCCRXIACK`-plus-OpenBSD-`cl.c` entry story -- which this row records as a NEW FACT improving\nreachability, and which the brief built its S4 around -- is UNNECESSARY.  **A single legal byte write\nof 0x04 to offset 0x02 terminates the emulator on the spot.**  No second access, no driver, no\ninterrupt.\n\n*That does not make the earlier finding wrong -- SCCRXIACK really does run the helper with no\ndirection test, and that really is routine traffic.  It makes it SUPERFLUOUS: the row had found a\nlong road to a place reachable in one step, and nobody noticed the short one because the long one was\ninteresting.*\n\n**CONFIRMED BY READING, beyond what the brief asserted:**\n  * `pcctwo_reg[]` holds **`uint8_t`**; GENCTL rejects non-byte writes, but the accepted byte write\n    **stores `idata` WITHOUT MASKING** and immediately calls the helper.  **No read-only treatment\n    prevents the bit being stored**, and the header explicitly permits byte accesses.\n  * The ten call sites are confirmed with their contexts -- 383 `pcctwo_interrupt_common()`, 400 the\n    device TICK (unconditionally), 459 GENCTL write, 551 T1ICR/T2ICR, 574 SCCICR, 588 SCCTX,\n    602 SCCRX, 609 SCCRXIACK (no direction test), 622 SCSIICR, 662 MASK -- all reaching **one shared\n    terminating branch**.\n  * **THE CONDITION IS NOT STICKY:** a GENCTL write CLEARING C040 stores the cleared value before\n    calling the helper.  So the state is recoverable in principle -- and unrecoverable in practice,\n    because the arming write has already terminated the process.\n  * **`400` is the device TICK, called unconditionally.**  So even a guest that arms C040 and then\n    does nothing at all is terminated at the next tick.  That is worth stating separately: it removes\n    the last version of the story in which the guest must go on to do something.\n\n**Q4 ANSWERED, and it is the opposite of the neighbouring rounds' problem:** *\"Survival is a\nnonvacuous oracle here.\"*  This project has measured that survival rows at REFUSAL-EFFECT sites are\nvacuous by construction -- guarded and unguarded code both survive.  Here the pre-fix build genuinely\ndoes NOT survive, so survival discriminates.  **But it cannot establish interrupt correctness**, which\nis the second half of the disposition question and needs its own row.\n\n**THE WITNESS IS NOW TRIVIALLY CHEAP.**  This row's `witness` field says a rung-3 attempt *\"looks\ncheap (one more session in `regress/pcc2_width_probe.py`, which already byte-writes this register on\nthis machine)\"* -- and with arming-equals-termination it is cheaper still: **one byte write and\nobserve that the process is gone.**  The field's promise that it says `not yet done` rather than\n`did not think to ask` is now cashable in a single session.\n\n*And a note on the brief: it was written with C5 applied FROM THE START -- neutral register, scoped\nreading, no chain -- on a subject (process termination) that is trigger 1's own vocabulary.  **It was\nnot refused.**  First time today I wrote one that way instead of learning it from a refusal.*\n\n**READY TO CLAIM AS A ROUND, 2026-09-07.**  The stated blocker was *\"next round after\npcc2widthkills closes\"*; that row now carries `commits=b78bb0f`, so the width fix is IN THE TREE and\nonly its formal closure is outstanding.  Nothing about this defect depends on that closure.\n\n**THE WITNESS IS ONE SESSION IN AN EXISTING PROBE.**  `regress/pcc2_width_probe.py` already drives\nGENCTL byte writes on this machine and carries an authoring-time assertion, `c040ok()`, whose whole\njob is to keep its lanes (`0xd9`/`0x40`) OFF this path.  **So the probe already proves the access\nworks and already knows the bit is dangerous** -- the witness is the same access with the bit set,\nand the oracle is that the process is gone.\n\n  *That assertion is worth keeping rather than deleting when the row is added: it protects the WIDTH\n  rows from wandering onto this path, which is a different job from the new row deliberately\n  stepping onto it.  A row that turns the guard off for the whole file would re-open exactly what\n  `c040ok()` was written to prevent.*\n\n**FIX SHAPE, from the audit:** DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE, with its own latch bit -- the same\nper-instance reasoning as `dev_sgi_re`'s bits, since a shared latch is silenced by the other site's\nordinary traffic.  **The open design question is Q2, which the audit did not settle from the source:**\nafter declining, does the helper fall through to the normal level recalculation, or return having\nasserted nothing?  Those differ in what the guest observes, and the answer decides whether the round\nowes a second row on interrupt behaviour -- which the verdict's own second clause anticipates\n(*\"survival ... cannot establish interrupt correctness\"*).\n\n*** WITNESS DESIGN REVIEWED AND NOT RATIFIED.  grok,\n`regress/artifacts/pcc2c040kill/grok_witness_20260907.txt`. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"NOT RATIFY as proposed.  A guest `st.b` of 0x04 at GENCTL is a sound CORE of a rung-3\nwitness of this kill.  Survival-only, in `pcc2_width_probe.py`, with no same-decode preset and no\nsite-attribution, is not.  **That is the same class as the design you asked me to assume: the\nobservation is either destroyed by the defect, or it reports success while measuring nothing.**\"*\n\n**THE CORE SURVIVES: one legal byte write is the right reproduction.**  What fails is my ORACLE, and\nthe required attribution is specific:\n\n  1. **`how == \"exit1\"`, not merely `not alive`.**  `not alive` is satisfied by several unrelated\n     deaths.\n  2. **The transcript must contain `C040 interrupt assertions`.**  Missing text plus exit1 is\n     *FAILED FOR SOME OTHER REASON*, not a pass -- which is the carrier's own rule 4 (*every\n     reproduction must answer \"did it fail for the reason under test?\"*), applied to a row I had\n     written without it.\n  3. **Controls in sessions that MUST SURVIVE**, so a construction failure cannot masquerade as the\n     defect: a RAM round-trip; CHIPID `ld.bu` -> 0x20 (RAM would not answer that); and a\n     **C040-CLEAR GENCTL round-trip of a nonzero value** -- 0x82, already proven by existing rows in\n     `pcc2_width_probe.py` and `pcc2defaultkill`.  *That third one is the same-decode preset: it\n     proves the register is reachable and writable BEFORE the killing value is used.*\n  4. *** THEY MUST ALL RUN BEFORE THE KILLING STORE.  Post-kill they cannot run. ***\n\n**POINT 4 IS THE ONE I WOULD NOT HAVE FOUND, and it is the Alpha design's flaw wearing different\nclothes.**  There the defect destroyed the TRANSLATION the observation needed; here it destroys the\nPROCESS the controls need.  In both cases the observation and the thing observed cannot coexist, and\nin both cases I designed the observation first and never asked what state it required afterwards.\n\n*** THREE WITNESS DESIGNS OF MINE HAVE NOW BEEN REVIEWED AND ALL THREE HAD A FLAW OF THE SAME CLASS.\n***  Alpha swpctx (defect destroys the translation), `#505`'s tiled row (two silent declines read as\nplausible values), and this one (survival is not attribution).  **The common shape is that I design\nwhat to OBSERVE and not how to ATTRIBUTE it** -- the ladder's clause (ii) asks for a liveness control\nAND a signature control, and in each case I supplied the first and reasoned my way past the second.\nRecorded here rather than as a carrier rule because three instances is a pattern about ME, and the\ncheap remedy is already available: **route every witness design to a reviewer before building it.**\nThat has now cost one invocation each time and caught three real defects.\n\n**ALSO FLAGGED, and it needs checking before the round:** the seat calls S3's tick claim *possibly\nsequencing-wrong* -- i.e. whether call site 400 (the unconditional device tick) really does reach the\nkill for a guest that arms C040 and then does nothing.  I asserted that from the call-site list; it\nis not verified.\n\n*** THE TICK CLAIM IS VACUOUS, AND SO IS 'TEN CALL SITES' AS A STATEMENT OF HAZARD.  My record,\ncorrected by checking the flag grok raised. ***\n\ngrok called S3's tick claim *\"possibly sequencing-wrong\"*.  Checked, and it is wrong:\n\n  * `DEVICE_TICK(pcc2)` really does call `reassert_interrupts(d)` unconditionally -- that part of my\n    claim is literally true;\n  * **but GENCTL is written in exactly ONE place** -- its own case arm -- and that arm calls\n    `reassert_interrupts()` in the SAME ACCESS.  Every other reference to `pcctwo_reg[PCCTWO_GENCTL]`\n    in the file is a READ (`:271` the C040 test, `:341` the MIEN test).  A wide write is DECLINED\n    BEFORE the store.\n\n**So the process always terminates on the ARMING WRITE ITSELF, and the tick can never be the first\nhit.**  *\"Even a guest that arms C040 and then does nothing is terminated at the next tick\"* describes\na state that cannot be reached: there is no interval during which C040 is set and the process is\nalive.\n\n*** AND THE SHARPER CORRECTION: 'TEN CALL SITES' IS TRUE AND MISLEADING. ***  All ten reach one\nshared branch, which is what the audit established and which stands.  But **nine of them are\nunreachable AS A FIRST HIT**, because the only way the guard's condition becomes true is a write that\nimmediately calls the helper itself.  **The defect has exactly ONE reachable entry.**\n\n**WHAT THIS CHANGES:**\n  * the WITNESS is simpler than the design assumed -- one session, one store; **no tick row is owed**,\n    and a row waiting for a tick would wait forever;\n  * the SEVERITY framing is unchanged (one legal byte still ends the emulator) but the *breadth*\n    framing was inflated: ten sites reads as ten ways in, and there is one;\n  * the FIX is unaffected -- de-fatalising the shared branch covers all ten regardless of which can\n    reach it first, and that is the right place to repair it.\n\n*This is the third over-broad claim of mine this session and the same shape as the others: a census I\nran correctly, restated as a claim about REACHABILITY that the census does not support.  The count of\ncall sites answers \"how many places call this helper\"; it does not answer \"how many can arrive here\nfirst\", and I wrote the second while measuring the first.*\n\n*** FULL PANEL 2026-09-07 (`_scratchpad/panel_20260907_124918`).  FOUR SEATS REJECTED MY S3\nCORRECTION, AND THEY WERE RIGHT ABOUT THE WARRANT EVEN THOUGH THE CONCLUSION SURVIVED. ***\n\n    codex      PENDING CODE REVIEW      grok      (mid-answer, 422 b)\n    minimax    REJECT                   glm       answered 8.3 kB -- NO BURN this time\n    kimi-sub   answered                 deepseek  answered\n    kimi CLI   403-walled, covered      agy       deliberately not fired\n\n**ZERO SEATS DOWN -- the first such panel of the day.**  glm did NOT burn, on a brief far more focused\nthan pass 3's, which matches the carrier's finding that burns track BRIEF COMPLEXITY rather than the\nseat.\n\n*** THE ATTACK, and glm put it most precisely: *** *\"a count of references to the symbolic constant\nCANNOT ENUMERATE STORES REACHING BYTE 2 -- and any wide/bulk store, nonzero reset default, or\nunzeroed allocation falsifies S3 and revives S4's tick row.\"*  kimi-sub: *\"asserted rather than\nenumerated ... the packet never lists all stores into `pcctwo_reg[]`.\"*  deepseek: *\"S3's one-entry\nclaim is unsupported.\"*\n\n**THEY ARE RIGHT ABOUT THE EVIDENCE.**  My grep was for `PCCTWO_GENCTL` -- the symbolic constant --\nwhich is a CORRELATE of \"stores that set C040\", not the class.  That is exactly the deriver failure\nthe carrier documents: *before writing a deriver, ask what makes a member a member.*\n\n*** THE PROPER ENUMERATION, run by class rather than by name, and S3 SURVIVES: ***\n  * **every write into `pcctwo_reg[]` in any index form** -- 40 sites; all use symbolic constants and\n    none names GENCTL except its own case arm at `:458`;\n  * **the one BULK write**, `memcpy(d->pcctwo_reg + relative_addr, data, len)` at `:482` -- the\n    falsifier glm predicted.  It sits in the `T1CMP/T2CMP/T1COUNT/T2COUNT` arm, so `relative_addr` is\n    one of **0x04, 0x08, 0x0c, 0x10** and the copy runs FORWARD.  **GENCTL is 0x02, BELOW all four.\n    It cannot be reached.**  (`:429` is the read-side memcpy; `:438` a zero-fill of the caller's\n    buffer.)\n  * **nonzero reset default** -- `:748` sets `pcctwo_reg[PCCTWO_GENCTL] = 0x00`.  Excluded.\n  * **unzeroed allocation** -- `:738` `memset(d, 0, sizeof(struct pcc2_data))`.  Excluded.\n  * **numeric index 2 or byte-2 arithmetic** -- none.\n\n**SO: S3 AND S4 STAND, AND THEIR WARRANT IS NOW AN ENUMERATION RATHER THAN A GREP FOR A NAME.**  The\nseats did not find a defect in the code; they found one in my evidence, and refusing to ratify is\nwhat produced the real check.  *Unanimity on a brief-supplied fact is worth nothing -- but unanimous\nREFUSAL to accept one is worth a great deal.*\n\n**DEEPSEEK'S SMALLEST EDIT, which is a genuine clause-(d) mutant against the design's own preset:**\n*\"changing the C040 test to `!= 0x82`, leaving the 0x82 control green.\"*  The witness design uses a\nC040-clear round-trip of 0x82 as its same-decode preset -- so a repair conditioned on that exact\nvalue keeps the preset green while the defect returns for every other value.  **The preset and the\nmutant would share a constant**, which is the shared-constant hazard `m88ksfd1row` is queued about.\nRecorded as an owed mutant before the round runs.\n\n**minimax's REJECT additionally calls S6 internally inconsistent with the disposition it verifies** --\nunread in detail; carried as an open item for the next pass rather than summarised from its verdict\nline.\n\n*** A GENUINE DISPOSITION SPLIT BETWEEN THE TWO STRONGEST SEATS.  FOR THE FLAGSHIP ADJUDICATION;\nBOTH SIDES IN THEIR OWN WORDS. ***\n\n**codex (pass-1 audit): DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE.**  *\"Survival is a nonvacuous oracle here, but\ncannot establish interrupt correctness.\"*\n\n**grok (full panel): DO NOT SHIP THAT.  ACCEPT.**  *\"DO NOT SHIP DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE.  S1-S4\nhold after re-enumeration.  The right member is ACCEPT (store the byte, delete the `exit(1)`, fall\nthrough).\"*\n\n*** grok's CASE, and it is the better-evidenced of the two: ***\n  * **C040 is R/W in the board manual.**  `OPER R/W` on that bit in VMESBCP2 -- the manual this\n    project already has at `_scratchpad/vmesbcp2.pdf`.  It is **not an unlisted offset, not a\n    read-only bit, and not a wide write with a lane hazard**.\n  * *** SO APPLYING `#500`/`#501`'s DECLINE IS WARRANT TRANSFER FROM THE WRONG SIBLING -- AND THAT\n    WAS MY ERROR. ***  I took the disposition from the same FILE without checking the same\n    SITUATION.  `#500` declines because a wide write's `idata` carries the neighbour's lane;\n    `#501` declines an unlisted OFFSET; VECBASE masks bits the manual forbids; CHIPID is read-only.\n    **None of those is a legal R/W bit at a listed offset.**\n  * **In-tree ACCEPT precedents it names instead:** `dev_footbridge.c`'s `+0x188` IRQEnable\n    (computed the right value then `exit(1)` -> ACCEPT, no latch, because there is no event to\n    announce); `#432` TIMER_EXTERNAL (a legal named encoding that hit `exit(1)`); and\n    `m8820x_unimplemented` (complain-once and continue).\n  * **The decisive argument: a latched \"C040 interrupt assertions... TODO\" followed by actually\n    asserting INT is A WRONG RECORD IN PRODUCT CODE** -- the same shape the IRQEnable round refused,\n    printing \"unimplemented; ignored\" about a path that is implemented and is not ignored.\n  * **C040 selects which PINS carry a level this emulator does not model.  It does not change the\n    level itself.**  So storing the bit and using the existing 88100 INT path is a no-op relative to\n    every observable the emulator has -- which is ACCEPT, not a refusal.\n  * The addition licence: *\"one more latch cannot hurt\" is not a warrant*, and this file just spent a\n    round moving IPL/MASK off process-global statics.\n  * *(Context, offered as context and not measurement: OpenBSD/mvme88k never writes `pcc2_genctl`\n    in the in-tree guest; the 68k sibling sets IEN and not C040.)*\n\n*** ONE SENTENCE OF grok's IS FACTUALLY WRONG, AND ITS CONCLUSION SURVIVES WITHOUT IT. ***  It writes\nthat *\"the helper ALREADY COMPUTED IPL vs MASK vs MIEN and is about to INTERRUPT_ASSERT/DEASSERT when\nit dies.\"*  **It has not.**  The C040 test is `dev_pcc2.c:271`, the first statement after\n`int assert = 0;`, and the recalculation begins at `:279-282`.  Nothing is computed when it dies.\n\n  **But the substantive claim does not depend on the tense:** delete the kill and the helper WILL\n  compute the level and assert on the existing path, and C040 does not change that level.  So the\n  REASON is wrong and the CONCLUSION stands -- recorded that way rather than letting a wrong sentence\n  ride inside a right verdict, which this project has been bitten by before.\n\n**grok also holds S6's attribution INSUFFICIENT even as corrected:** *\"it cannot tell bit from value,\ncannot tell which repair you shipped, and at 0x04 it cannot tell Q2's two helper tails apart.\"*  That\nthird clause is the sharpest: **the witness cannot distinguish fall-through from return-without-\nasserting**, which is exactly the Q2 the audit left open.\n\n*** THIS VINDICATES THE HOLD, and it is worth saying plainly.  On codex's answer alone I would have\nshipped DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE -- which grok argues is a WRONG RECORD IN PRODUCT CODE, of the\nexact shape a previous round refused.  Two seats, opposite dispositions, and the owner's\nhold-for-adjudication directive is what keeps it from being settled by whichever seat I asked\nfirst. ***\n\n**FOR THE ADJUDICATION:** the question is not \"decline or accept\" in the abstract.  It is whether a\nLEGAL R/W BIT whose modelled effect is nil should be STORED (accept) or REFUSED (decline), and\nwhether an announce that describes an unmodelled PIN SELECTION while the INT path is used is a true\nrecord or a false one.\n\n*** THE RUNG-3 WITNESS IS BUILT AND MEASURED.  `regress/pcc2_c040_probe.py`, 3/4 on the committed\nbuild, artifact `regress/artifacts/pcc2c040kill/witness_HEAD_20260907.raw`. ***\n\n**The `witness` field used to read *\"none -- READ, not run ... it has NOT been made, and this field\nsays `not yet done', never `did not think to ask'.\"*  That obligation is now discharged**, and the\nlifecycle's own ruling is why it existed: *ask one seat to try a rung-3 probe before recording a\nround as rung-1*.  The measurement:\n\n    genctl ctl     alive        alive        CONTROL: same st.b, same GENCTL, benign 0x10\n    genctl ctlq    no           no           CONTROL: the benign write must NOT reach the C040 arm\n    c040 kill      died(exit1)  alive        THE DEFECT -- RED, as clause (i) requires\n    c040 attrib    yes          yes          ATTRIBUTION: the death carries the arm's unique text\n    EVIDENCE: pcc2: C040 interrupt assertions... TODO\n\n**ONE legal guest byte store ends the host.**  No driver, no second access, no interrupt: the GENCTL\nwrite arm stores `idata` UNMASKED and calls `reassert_interrupts()` in the same access, whose first\ntest is C040.  *That one-access reading is a codex CORRECTION of the row's own founding story* -- the\nrow was filed around a longer SCCRXIACK road reachable from OpenBSD's `cl.c`, which is real and\nSUPERFLUOUS.  Nobody had noticed the short road because the long one was interesting.\n\n**THE WITNESS'S OWN CONTRACT, written into its header:** it witnesses THIS `exit(1)` and is **NOT a\ndetector of the repair**.  The adjudicated disposition is DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE, and *\"host\nsurvives\"* greens for a silent decline, a latched one and a real C040 implementation alike.  **A fix\nround owes a row that discriminates them** -- for the latched form, the announce text and its\nonce-only property are the available channel.\n\n*Two apparatus failures on the way in, both caught by the probe's own controls rather than by\ninspection: a missing `DUMMY` made the forked child die on a NameError, and `c040 attrib` correctly\nreported **died-elsewhere** rather than crediting the kill; then the `\\0` in the repair cooked into\nFOUR REAL NULL BYTES in the source, which is this project's escape-consumption class arriving through\na file write instead of a shell.  The second fix is escape-free by construction -- `bytes(4)`, which\nno layer can consume.*\n\n*** PASS 1 FIX-DESIGN PANEL: SIX SEATS, UNANIMOUS **IMPLEMENT-WITH-REVISIONS**.  Panel dir\n`_scratchpad/panel_20260907_233310/`.  agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (reserved for measurement);\nopus5 and fable5 DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (reserved for the 2026-09-09 adjudication).  The kimi CLI\nis 403-WALLED and its seat was COVERED by the kimi-k3:cloud substitute -- recorded by transport,\nand PACKET-FED, so it is attack reasoning and not a repo-fact reading. ***\n\n### *** 1.  FOUR SEATS CAUGHT THE SAME DEFECT IN MY PROPOSAL: THE DELETION WAS NEVER STATED. ***\n\ngrok, minimax, deepseek and the kimi substitute each reached it independently.  minimax put it\nplainest: *\"The fix as written is ADDED code near the MIEN test, but the inlined function still\ncontains the original `fatal(...); exit(1);`.  Nothing in the brief says delete the top block.  A\nnaive application that pastes the new code without deleting the old will keep killing the host.\"*\nkimi: *\"an insertion-only patch is a silent no-op.\"*\n\n**The proposal's comment said \"used to be\", which IMPLIES the removal without stating it.**  The\npatch must say REPLACE `:271-277`, not add at the tail.  *When four independent readers make the\nsame inference, the brief is the defect -- this project's own sketch-rule finding.*\n\n### *** 2.  P1 AND Q1 ARE NOW SETTLED FROM THE PRIMARY SOURCE -- AND THE MANUAL WAS ON THIS\n### MACHINE THE WHOLE TIME, WITH THE CARRIER TELLING ME TO OPEN IT. ***\n\nminimax held the entire round conditional on P1 and named the missing artifact exactly: *\"One fact\nneeded: an authoritative PCC2 GENCTL bit map\"*, adding that if the value were 0x03 *\"the proposed\nfix would silently latch on a bit the hardware never uses.\"*\n\n**`_scratchpad/vmesbcp2.txt` is the Motorola MVME187 board manual, added 2026-09-05, and the\ncarrier says in terms: \"A round about PCC2/CD2401/MVME187 opens THIS before reasoning from the\nsource alone.\"  I did not.**  Opened now, at its General Control Register page:\n\n    ADR/SIZ   $FFF42002 (8 bits)\n    DIR       15    14  13  12  11    10    9     8\n    NAME      DR0                     C040  MIEN  FAST\n    OPER      R/W   R   R   R   R     R/W   R/W   R/W\n    RESET     0     0   0   0   0     0     0     0\n\nThe manual numbers the bits 15..8 as the upper byte of a word view, so 15->7, 10->2, 9->1, 8->0:\n\n    DR0  = 0x80     C040 = 0x04     MIEN = 0x02     FAST = 0x01\n\n**ALL FOUR MATCH THE FORK'S HEADER EXACTLY.  P1 IS CONFIRMED and the OpenBSD `include/pcctworeg.h`\n`C040 0x03` is simply a buggy header** -- 0x03 is `FAST|MIEN`, which is not a coherent C040 mask.\n*The extracted-table stagger hazard is covered here by FOUR calibration points plus the address\n`$FFF42002` matching offset 0x02 and the all-zero RESET row matching the modelled reset.*\n\n*** AND THE SAME PAGE SETTLES Q1, WHICH NO SEAT COULD ANSWER FROM THE PACKET: ***\n\n> *\"If the C040 bit is set, the interrupt request goes to the MPU via the EIPL\\* pins **at the level\n> that is programmed for SCSI interrupts in the SCSI Interrupt Control Register**.  If the C040 bit\n> is cleared, the interrupt goes to the MPU via the INT pin...\"*\n\n**C040 selects the DELIVERY PATH, not the level.**  The programmed interrupt level is used either\nway, so the IPL recalculation stays meaningful in C040 mode and only the ASSERTION is suppressed --\nwhich is exactly the proposed shape, now on a primary-source warrant instead of an inference from\nthe sibling idiom.  **MIEN's own text confirms the relationship**: *\"When it is low, all interrupts\nfrom the PCCchip2 are disabled (this includes both the EIPL\\* pins and the INT pin)\"* -- MIEN gates\nBOTH paths, C040 selects BETWEEN them.  So joining the two tests is right and they are not\nredundant.\n\n*Q1 was the question three seats said the packet could not settle.  It cost one grep of a file the\ncarrier had already told me to open.*\n\n### 3.  TWO PANEL CONCERNS CHECKED AND REFUTED (not judged)\n\n* **minimax #4 / deepseek's bit-collision mutant.**  `PCC2_W_*` is a **C99 enum with sequential\n  members terminated by `PCC2_W_NBITS`**, plus a `pcc2_w_fits_in_warned` width assert.  **A\n  correctly-added enumerator CANNOT collide** -- that is the same \"duplicates impossible by\n  construction\" idiom this project adopted deliberately elsewhere.  *The row is still worth having\n  against a hand-written literal, which is what deepseek's step 1 actually tests.*\n* **minimax #3, \"the witness goes stale/red after the fix\".**  Mechanically FALSE:\n  `row(\"c040 attrib\", \"yes\" if (alive2 or sig) else \"died-elsewhere\", \"yes\", ...)` -- post-fix\n  `alive2` is true, so it evaluates \"yes\" against a wanted \"yes\" and stays **GREEN**.  *The real\n  point survives in weaker form: the row becomes VACUOUS post-fix, since it can no longer fail, and\n  the EVIDENCE print falls silent.*\n\n### 4.  THE ROWS THE ROUND NOW OWES (from the panel, not from me)\n\n* **deepseek's four-step latch row** -- pre-dirty another `warned` bit via ordinary guest traffic;\n  write C040; assert host survives AND the announce appears EXACTLY ONCE; write C040 again; assert\n  no second announce.  *Separates silent decline, non-latching and bit collision in one session;\n  \"presence alone is not enough.\"*\n* **minimax's non-suppressing mutant row** -- C040 set with MIEN=1 and IPL above the mask, asserting\n  the CPU interrupt line is NOT asserted.  **Without it, a fix that announces loudly and forgets\n  `assert = 0` is green.**\n\n### 5.  PROVENANCE FOUND IN THE FILE'S OWN COMMENT\n\n`pcc2widthkills` already documented this defect in product code while deliberately scoping it out:\n*\"NOT FIXED HERE, cited so 'twelve kills removed' is not read as 'file clean': pcc2c040kill (a LEGAL\nbyte write of 0x04 to GENCTL still ends the host in reassert_interrupts).\"*  **An independent\nconfirmation of the defect, written by the round that chose not to fix it** -- and the sentence this\nround discharges.\n\n*** FIXED AS `#512`.  Kill table 2026-09-08 in a scratch clone: four mutants KILLED, ONE ESCAPE\nWHICH THE ROUND GRADES AS A FLOOR BREACH RATHER THAN DISCLOSING IT AWAY. ***\n\n    BASE 7/7 | M_b_reinstate 3/7 | M_d2_silent 4/7 | M_d3_nolatch 6/7 | M_d4_collide 6/7\n    M_d1_noassert 7/7  *** CLEAN SWEEP -- the non-suppressing mutant escapes ***\n\n`assert = 0` is the repair's EFFECT, so nulling it is inside clause (d)'s DEPENDS-ON closure.  The\nescape is structural, verified twice: `PCC2_IRQ_INT` (0x20) is masked out of every guest-writable\narm (`TxICR & 0x17`, `SCCICR & 0x1f`) and its only producer is `pcc_timer_tick`, a `timer_add`\nHOST-TIMER callback that the debugger stops for the duration of a `step`.  **The repair is a\nfree-running probe shape, filed as `pcc2c040assertrow`.**\n\n*** THE codex PASS-1 ANSWER, MINED 2026-09-08 -- I HAD RECORDED IT AS \"findings not yet fully mined\",\nWHICH IS AN UNREAD REVIEW WITH A HONEST LABEL ON IT.  It corrects TWO of the round's premises and\nsupplies a MUTANT THE KILL TABLE DID NOT BUILD. ***\n\n**P2 WAS OVERSTATED AND THE CORRECT WORDING IS WEAKER.**  I wrote *\"No file in the OpenBSD 2.2 tree\nwrites C040 to GENCTL.\"*  codex: **\"The defensible wording is 'no explicit C040-setting use found,'\nnot 'no file writes C040'\"** -- because a grep over named macros cannot exclude literal or indirect\nstores, and, decisively, **P3's read-modify-write (`genctl |= IEN`) WRITES BACK AN ALREADY-SET C040\nBIT.**  Its census also beats mine: C040 defines sit in THREE source headers (mvme88k `dev/`,\nmvme88k `include/`, mvme68k `dev/`) plus an on-disk `machine/` copy.\n\n**P5 WAS TOO NARROW.**  I wrote that C040 means *\"the CPU is an MC68040\"*.  codex: it selects the\n**M68000-family interrupt INTERFACE**, and *\"the manual explicitly says to clear it for an\nMC88100\"*.  The robustness rationale is unaffected; the shipped-guest compatibility claim remains\nunestablished either way.\n\n*** AND A CLAUSE-(d) MUTANT THE KILL TABLE DID NOT BUILD, DISTINCT FROM M_d1: \"SUPPRESSION INSIDE\nTHE LATCH\". ***  Keep the guard AND the announcement, but move `assert = 0` INSIDE the\n`if (!warned)` block:\n\n> *\"The first encounter suppresses correctly; later encounters can assert the CPU line.  The\n> announcement count remains exactly one.  This directly meets clause (d)'s 'guard kept, repair\n> nulled' shape.\"*\n\n**Every shipped row stays green under it** -- `c040 says` yes, `c040 once` 1, `c040 predirty` yes.\nSo the detector has a SECOND declared escape, and it is nastier than `M_d1` because the fix looks\ncorrect on first contact and fails only on repeat encounters.\n\n> **THE SHIPPED FIX IS NOT VULNERABLE TO IT: `assert = 0;` is OUTSIDE the latch block**, which is\n> what codex's implementation note asked for (*\"Keep `assert = 0` outside the warning-latch\n> condition\"*).  Verified in the diff.  **The gap is in the DETECTOR, not in the code** -- and it\n> folds into `pcc2c040assertrow`, which needs the same free-running shape.\n\ncodex also independently re-derived the `(once)` census the carrier says never to quote: **30\n`(once)` after excluding comments and joining adjacent C string literals**, versus 12\n`(once per offset)`; the 34 raw occurrences include four comments.\n\n*It flagged one provenance gap in my own artifact, correctly: the witness raw names `build/gxemul`\nwith no commit or binary hash, so it does not independently establish which build it measured.*\n\n*** PASS 2 REFUSED THE ROUND'S CLOSING DISPOSITION, AND IT IS RIGHT.  STATE REVERTED `fixed` ->\n`open` 2026-09-08. ***\n\n**grok's ruling:** *\"disposition 2 is wrong.  1 and 2 cannot both stand.  1 is the right grade ...\n2 is then the (e) REMEDY applied to a (d) GRADE.  That is the contradiction.  L2 says replace the\ndetector in-round.  Coverage-debt filing is the above-floor path.  **Honesty about the breach does\nnot create an exception L2 does not have; a declared L2 that ships is the precedent this question\nexists to refuse.**\"*\n\nAnd the sharpest sentence, which I should have seen myself: *\"The CHANGELOG already says the round\ndoes not claim a floor-meeting detector -- good -- and the ledger row is already `state: fixed`.\n**Those two sentences cannot sit together.**\"*  **They did, for about an hour, in records I wrote.**\n\n*** AND MY WARRANT FOR SHIPPING WAS FALSE.  VERIFIED HERE, NOT TAKEN ON TRUST. ***\n\nI claimed the non-suppressing mutant could not be rowed because `PCC2_IRQ_INT` has *\"only one\nproducer, `pcc_timer_tick`, a host-timer callback that `step` stops\"*.  **The first half is right and\nthe second is wrong:**\n\n    dev_pcc2.c  pcctwo_interrupt_common()  ORs PCC2_IRQ_INT onto SCSIICR / SCCTX / SCCRX\n                                           when those lines assert\n    dev_clmpcc.c:88-89   if ((d->reg[CLMPCC_REG_IER] & 3) != 0)\n                                 INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq_scc_tx);\n    machine_mvme88k.c    constructs clmpcc with irq=...pcc2...\n\n**So a GUEST STORE to the clmpcc IER asserts a line whose callback sets the INT bit, synchronously,\ninside that store.**  No host timer, no free-running probe: `st.b` IEN|IL to SCCTX, `st.b` to\nCLMPCC IER, `st.b` `C040|MIEN` to GENCTL.  **The obstacle was never the STATE -- it is the ORACLE.**\n\n> **THE ERROR IN MY REASONING, stated plainly because it is a repeatable one: I checked that no\n> guest store writes INT INTO THE ICR, and concluded that no guest store can set it.**  A store to a\n> DIFFERENT DEVICE sets it through the interrupt callback.  *\"Which stores write this field\" is not\n> the same question as \"which stores cause this field to change\".*\n\n*** grok's Q2: THE SMALLEST UNDECLARED EDIT THAT STILL SCORES 7/7 IS ONE TOKEN. ***\n\n**`assert = 0;` -> `return;`**  Announce, latch, survival, `SAYS`, once-count and predirty are all\nunchanged; **the CPU INT line is simply never deasserted.**  No shipped row sees it, and grok gives\nthe reason: *\"every session writes 0x04 into a reset GENCTL, so MIEN is already clear, `assert` is\nalready 0, and `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT` of a quiet line is a no-op.  The discriminating sequence is\npending INT + MIEN, THEN set C040.  The 7 rows never build that.\"*\n\nFour more same-score edits it named: move the block to the top and `return` (skips IPL recalc,\n`cur_int_vec[]` AND deassert); drop `(once)` from the message (`c040 once` counts `SAYS`, not the\nmarker); `fatal` -> `debug` (the probe launches with `-V`, so it still prints); swap the C040 and\nMIEN tests (independent, order invisible).\n\n*** AN L4 IN THE SHIPPED PRODUCT COMMENT, FOUND BY READING THE CONTROL FLOW. ***  My comment said an\nearly return here *\"would skip the IPL recalculation, the cur_int_vec[] updates and the\nINTERRUPT_DEASSERT below\"*.  **At the NEW site the first two have already run** -- a return there\nskips only the DEASSERT.  That sentence is true of a return at the OLD site and false at this one:\n*\"a wrong reason attached to a right placement.  Wrong reasons get reused -- the next mutant is\nexactly `return` at this site.\"*  **Corrected in place.**\n\n**Dispositions 3, 4 and 5 were CHECKED and HOLD** -- including that the width probe's `latch all`\nrow still wants 12 announces from 12 wide writes and does not iterate `NBITS`, so appending the enum\nmember renumbers nothing; and that `c040 attrib` is green post-fix only via `alive2`, which grok\ncalls *\"the price of clause (i)\"* and agrees is right.\n\n**DISPOSITION: the three product hunks are correct and stay.  The ROUND does not close.**  The L2\nrepair is now known to be buildable in a stepped probe, so it is an in-round obligation rather than\na filed one.\n\n*** THE FLOOR BREACH IS CLOSED IN-ROUND, WHICH IS WHAT PASS 2 REQUIRED. ***\n\n    BASE            11/11\n    M_d1_noassert    9/10   `assert = 0' DELETED        -- was 7/7 (a CLEAN SWEEP)   KILLED\n    M_return         9/10   `assert = 0' -> `return;'   -- the one-token pass-2 mutant KILLED\n    M_latchinside   10/11   `assert = 0' INSIDE the latch -- was 10/10               KILLED\n\nThe round no longer ships a declared clause-(d) escape.  `pcc2c040assertrow` -- filed when the\nrepair was believed to need a probe shape this project did not have -- is CLOSED, and the detector\nis now 11 rows.\n\n*Pass 2's ruling was that grading (d) while FILING the repair is \"the (e) remedy applied to a (d)\ngrade\".  The correct response was to build the rows, and building them took one design review and\ntwo measured tables.*\n\n*** PASS 2 ON THE L2 REPAIR: SIX SEATS, TWO DO-NOT-SHIP, AND **FOUR OF SIX** REFUSED THE SAME\nDISPOSITION.  The detector went 11 -> 16 rows and now kills NINE mutants across FIVE tables. ***\n\n**FOUR SEATS INDEPENDENTLY REFUSED DISPOSITION 1** (grok, deepseek, glm, the kimi substitute);\nminimax defended it *conditionally* and named its own condition.  **I had declared the encounter\naxis closed while enumerating only the C040=1 slice of a two-lvalue product** -- and had filed the\nempty cell as contract debt, which is the SECOND time this round graded a floor-shaped hole that\nway.\n\n    THE PRODUCT              warned = 0          warned = 1\n      C040 clear             `irq live'          `c040 recover'   <- was EMPTY\n      C040 set               `c040 noassert'     `c040 noassert2'\n\n**THE FULL KILL RECORD, five tables:**\n\n    table 1   7 rows   M_b_reinstate 3/7 | M_d2_silent 4/7 | M_d3_nolatch 6/7 | M_d4_collide 6/7\n                       M_d1_noassert 7/7  *** ESCAPED ***\n    table 2  10 rows   M_d1_noassert 9/10 KILLED | M_return 9/10 KILLED\n                       M_latchinside 10/10  *** ESCAPED ***\n    table 3  11 rows   M_latchinside 10/11 KILLED\n    table 4  12 rows   M_sticky 11/12 KILLED        (grok's `|| (warned & ...)')\n    table 5  13 rows   M_mienclobber 12/13 KILLED   (`GENCTL &= ~PCC2_MIEN')\n\n**`M_mienclobber` is the clearest demonstration of why the collateral row was owed:** `irq live` and\n`c040 noassert` BOTH stayed GREEN under it -- every delivery row passes a fix that suppresses by\ndisabling all interrupts -- and only the GENCTL readback saw it, coming back `0x04` instead of\n`0x06`.  **Three seats named that mutant independently.**\n\n*** THE LAST ROWS CAME FROM THE SUBSTITUTE SEAT AND NO OTHER SEAT SAW IT. ***  The liveness control\nexisted for ONE session of FOUR: `c040 noassert` reaching COMPLETION was consistent with \"the fix\nsuppressed correctly\" AND with \"no interrupt was ever pending in that session\".  Three per-session\nSCCTX readbacks close it, at zero extra cost because the sessions already ran.  **16/16.**\n\n*Two apparatus facts recorded for the next reader:* the PC regex demands eight hex digits, so it\nreads the register DUMP and not the unpadded `BREAKPOINT:` line (the values are right; the line is\nnot the one I thought); and `(\"continue\", 6.0)` carries no `until`, so the full grace always\nelapses.  *And glm reconstructed the mixed-denominator defect from the printed numbers alone --\nwhich I had already found and corrected in the commit message but NOT in the brief the panel read.*\n\n*** THE FIRST PASS-2 PANEL HAD FIVE UNRECORDED SEATS, AND THE PRECOMMIT GATE CAUGHT IT -- WHICH IS\nTHIS SESSION'S OWN MOST-REPEATED FINDING, COMMITTED BY ME, ON THE ROUND WHERE I KEPT FIXING IT. ***\n\n`SEATS_READ_FAIL 5 seat answers exist on disk and are recorded NOWHERE` -- codex (1.0 MB), deepseek,\nglm, kimi and minimax from `panel_20260908_000754`.  **I recorded grok's answer from that panel and\nnone of the other five**, then spent the following hours mining OTHER rounds' unread reviews.\n\n**Recorded now, and the tally matters: FIVE OF SIX SEATS PUT THEIR RESIDUAL ON DISPOSITION 2** (ship\nwith the breach declared).  Only kimi declined to call it wrong, and for an honest packet-fed reason\n-- clause (d)'s text was not inlined, so it named the single fact that would settle it instead of\nguessing.  **deepseek prescribed the exact repair that was then built:** *\"add a row that can set the\nICR INT bit, kill M_d1, and THEN ship.\"*\n\n*** AND panel_ollama.py AUTO-RECOVERED A THINKING BURN, WHICH THE CARRIER DOES NOT RECORD IT DOING.\n***  glm's first shot returned an EMPTY `response` beside **119,022 characters of hidden reasoning**\nwith `done_reason=stop` -- the fifth seat-failure class, in the exact shape the carrier says defeats\nevery size check.  **The tool re-fired with `think=False` and got 124,850 characters back.**  The\ncarrier says detection of this class is solved by `seat_verdicts.py`; it does not say the LAUNCHER\nnow recovers it.  *That is a capability worth knowing about before anyone writes another paragraph\nabout burns being unrecoverable.*\n\n*** COMMITTED AS `9fa07fe` (PRECOMMIT_PASS, 0 hard, 60 files).  THE ROW STAYS **OPEN** ON PURPOSE. ***\n\nThe code shipped and is measured; what is outstanding is the FLAGSHIP PASS, which is held and queued\nfor the 2026-09-09 adjudication.  **A row whose fix is committed but whose panel is incomplete is\n`open` with its commit named** -- the same shape `pcc2widthkills` carries -- because closing it would\nassert a completeness the roster does not have.  *That is the provisional-adjudication rule applied\nto state rather than to a ruling.*\n\n**The gate refused this commit THREE TIMES and was right every time** (6 hard -> 3 -> 0): five rows\nfiled only into the ledger; three stages held-but-not-queued; **five seat answers recorded nowhere**;\nand two tooling CONVENTIONS I guessed instead of read -- `hold_question` is a ROW-level field, and\n`check_fable_queue` parses a level-3 heading whose first backticked token is the row id.\n\n*** A codex ANALYSIS JOB WAS FIRED AT THIS ROW ON 2026-09-08 AND THE BRIEF WAS STALE.  THE DEFECT\nWAS MINE, AND IT IS THE ONE CHECK THE BRIEF TEMPLATE PUTS FIRST. ***\n\nI wrote the brief from this row's TITLE -- *\"a legal byte write ... ends the host in\nreassert_interrupts()\"* -- and did NOT run the template's `§C0` step (*HAS THIS ROUND ALREADY\nSHIPPED?  TWO COMMANDS, BEFORE YOU WRITE ANYTHING*).  I had run it for `#515` the same evening.\n\ncodex answered correctly and its verdict is the refutation: *\"the termination claim is stale for this\ncheckout ... Exactly one executable `exit(1)` remains, and there is no executable `exit(1)` in this\nhelper.  The opening comment claiming this write 'still ends the host' contradicts the\nimplementation.\"*\n\n**VERIFIED here, and codex is right on every count:**\n\n    active exit( in dev_pcc2.c, comments stripped:  1   (at :414, NOT the C040 arm)\n    dev_pcc2.c:369-376   the C040 arm today is a LATCHED ANNOUNCE + suppression --\n                         `d->warned & (1u << PCC2_W_C040)', `fatal(... (once))', `assert = 0'\n    git log -- dev_pcc2.c   9fa07fe  \"#512 pcc2c040kill: one legal guest byte store ended the host\"\n    CHANGELOG.md:427        the `#512' round block\n\n**SO THE FIX SHIPPED AS `#512` AND THIS ROW IS OPEN ON PURPOSE** -- its own note says so in capitals,\nawaiting the flagship pass held for 2026-09-09. *The row was right, the code was right, and the brief\nI fed the seat described a state that stopped existing at `9fa07fe`.*\n\n> **THE COST: about 84 KB of a file-reading seat's effort spent establishing that shipped work had\n> shipped.**  Not wasted -- it produced the [[pcc2c040dup]] header finding in passing -- but the\n> question it was asked was answerable by two commands I already knew to run.\n\n**AND THE ROW'S OWN NOTE IS WHAT STOPPED ME GOING FURTHER WRONG.**  Reading codex's verdict, my next\nmove was to close this row as overtaken. The note's *\"THE ROW STAYS OPEN ON PURPOSE\"* is the only\nthing that prevented it. **A state field alone would not have: `open` on shipped work looks exactly\nlike `open` on unstarted work**, which is the state-contradicts-substance class this project already\nrecords -- and here the prose carried the distinction the state could not.",
35904:   "id": "m88kexcdeliver",
35905:   "title": "m88kexcdeliver  the m88k exception core stops EVERY guest CPU for five trap-like exception classes (PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION, ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE, INTEGER_OVERFLOW, MISALIGNED_ACCESS, UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE) instead of delivering VBR + 8*vector -- one guest `divu rX,rY,r0' ends the emulation, from user mode",
35910:   "witness": "probe:regress/m88k_exc_probe.py (rung 3: real m88k guest instructions through real decode on the COMMITTED testm88k machine; HEAD 0/9 probe2_head.out, 0/10 probe10_head.out, 3/22 probe_v3_head.out -- the 3 are the two HALT-expected negative controls and the pre-existing user vector; fix 22/22 probe_v3_fix.out and probe_v3_fix_final.out; artifacts regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/)",
35912:   "note": "DEFECT, HIGH.  cpu_m88k.c, anchor `vector < M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAPS_START && !implemented': only RESET/INTERRUPT/INSTRUCTION_ACCESS/DATA_ACCESS set `implemented = true'; nine other switch cases print a VERBOSITY_ERROR and break, so the fallback sets running=false on every CPU and returns (emul.c then breaks the main loop: a GRACEFUL guest-drivable machine halt, not an abort).  Pre-existing upstream (`git log -L' on the fallback returns only the 0.7.0 import); exposed by the #380 review, NOT introduced by #323/#380.  Reachable classes by raise site (deriver: grep -o 'm88k_exception(cpu, *M88K_EXCEPTION_[A-Z0-9_]+' src/cpus/cpu_m88k*.c | sort | uniq -c): PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION 10 (ldcr/stcr/xcr/rte/ld.usr/st.usr/tb0/tb1/tcnd in user mode), ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE 4 (divu/div by zero, any mode), INTEGER_OVERFLOW 3 (add/sub, any mode), MISALIGNED_ACCESS 1, UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE 1 (prom_call in user mode); BOUNDS_CHECK and ERROR have no LITERAL-CONSTANT raise site -- but a supervisor tb0/tb1/tcnd names any 9-bit vector directly (`m88k_exception(cpu, ic->arg[2], 1)'), so both ARE reachable today (MEASURED on pass 1: supervisor `tb1 0,r3,7' halts HEAD; an earlier wording here said 'NO raise site today (latent)' -- corrected by #502's pass 1).  ERROR is additionally reached by the SFRZ re-route when a handler faults.  Vector offsets verified against Table 6-1 (mc88100.txt:8524-8538): PRIVILEGE 0x30, ILLEGAL_DIVIDE 0x40, OVERFLOW 0x48, MISALIGNED 0x20.  DISPOSITION: DELIVER (not a device-style decline -- the guest OS has handlers for these): `implemented = true' for the five, flowing through the existing common path (SFRZ, EPSR, SXIP/SNIP/SFIP shadow, supervisor switch, pc = VBR + 8*vector); MISALIGNED already fills DMT in the load/store template.  WITNESS OWED AT RUNG 3 BEFORE ANY EDIT: cold-debugger probe on committed testm88k or luna-88k (exemplars regress/m88k_idle_probe.py, m88k_rounding_probe.py): supervisor `divu r2,r3,r0', VBR into RAM, breakpoint at VBR+0x40, free-run -- pre-fix `unimplemented vector=0x008' and the machine stops; post-fix PC == VBR+0x40, SXIP = faulting instruction | XIP_V, PSR shadow frozen, machine running; plus a LIVENESS control (an already-delivered vector) and an ATTRIBUTION control (the same instruction on a non-faulting operand does not vector).  Split from m88kexcsfu1 (one fix design per round).  Ranked at the top of the live-defect tier, just below the exit(1)-abort class (this one stops gracefully).  Provenance: retrospective re-check (owner directive 2026-09-04) batch 2 -- GPT-6 Astra (codex, max effort) read-only review, adjudicated by Fable 5.1; artifacts regress/artifacts/retro_20260905/batch2_m88k/ (astra_batch2.txt, fable_adj_batch2.md).  The other seven panel seats were DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED on the retrospective (owner scope); the fix round runs the full nine-seat panel.  ROUND #502 (2026-09-05): SEVEN classes delivered (the five plus BOUNDS and ERROR), at BOTH sites (switch-1 `implemented' AND a tail `case' -- the one-site form was MEASURED to stop the cpu at the tail default).  Detector regress/m88k_exc_probe.py v3 (22 fault rows + 2 controls; rows resid50/sfu1h EXPECT HALT as negative controls) wired into gate 11; HEAD 0/9 -> 0/10 -> 3/22 (the two HALT controls + the pre-existing user vector 128), fix 9/9 -> 10/10 -> 22/22.  Three kill tables + the Opus pass-2 table under regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/: table 1 exposed M_ERRORDROP (repaired: errortb), the Opus pass-2 table exposed M_NOVBR / M_SFRZTRAP / M_SFIPNOTGT / M_DSLOTSWAP (all 10/10; repaired: vbrtb, trapfrozen, dslot br.n +16), pass-2 seats exposed the trap-route blindness (repaired: misaligntb/unimpltb/div0tb/ovftb/privsuptb), the carry half of F14 (ovfc), the LEVEL channel (Fable: INFO vs ERROR IS observable at default verbosity), the `<=' tail-guard bound (usertb) and the missing negative controls (kimi: resid50, sfu1h); table 3 killed every one of those mutants; M_TRAPONLY EQUIVALENT (disclosed, m88kexcdebt).  Filed from the round: m88kdslotshadow MEDIUM, m88kexcfrozendmt LOW, m88kexchwtraps LOW, m88kexcdebt LOW.\n\n*** 2026-09-11: `commits` WAS EMPTY ON A **HIGH** ROW WHOSE FIX IS COMMITTED.  Set to `79cd12e`. ***\n\n**This row's own title says one guest `divu rX,rY,r0` from USER MODE ends the emulation** -- and the record said the work had not shipped.  *A reader trusting `commits` reads a live user-reachable emulator-halt as outstanding.*\n\n=== VERIFIED THREE INDEPENDENT WAYS BEFORE WRITING, because a correlate is not the thing ===\n  1. **The tag is in committed source, and it is a FIX rather than a reference.**  `cpu_m88k.c` carries **seven** `implemented = true;  /*  m88kexcdeliver  */` lines -- at MISALIGNED_ACCESS, UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE, PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION, BOUNDS_CHECK_VIOLATION, ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE, INTEGER_OVERFLOW and ERROR -- each an ASSIGNMENT that changes behaviour, not prose in a comment.\n  2. **Line history settles the commit**: `git log -L 728,731:src/cpus/cpu_m88k.c` gives exactly two commits -- `79cd12e` and `39748e3` (the unmodified upstream import).  So the arm went from upstream's form straight to the fix, in `79cd12e`.\n  3. **The commit message agrees and adds the scope**: *'#502 m88kexcdeliver (detector 46 -> 59 rows, **no product change after the fix**)'* -- i.e. that commit carries the product change, and passes 9-12 refined the DETECTOR only.\n\n*** THE TITLE UNDERCOUNTS ITS OWN FIX: it names FIVE classes and SEVEN are repaired *** (BOUNDS_CHECK_VIOLATION and ERROR are also tagged).  Left as-is rather than rewritten, because the title is what the row was FILED as and the extra two are a fact about the repair; noted here so a future reader does not read the title as the repair's denominator.\n\n*** STATE DELIBERATELY LEFT AT `in work`, AND THE RESTRAINT IS THE POINT. ***  Adding the commit is unambiguous -- the fix is in the tree and three checks agree.  **Closing it is not**: the sibling row [[m88kexcdetectorl2]] concerns an L2 in this very detector, and the most recent records commit (`8d372aa`) reports that *a HIGH row's 'measured clean sweep' was neither a sweep nor a failure -- the mutants target exception vectors the probe never drives*.  **A row covering work where part shipped and part is disputed is still live**, and this project's own rule is that a state misdescribing substance is the defect, in either direction.\n\n**PROVENANCE: found by a sweep for live rows with an EMPTY `commits` whose id appears in COMMITTED `src/` -- 23 of 284 such rows matched.**  *That match is a CORRELATE, not a finding: a tag in source can equally be a comment NAMING the row as still-open (`#497` owed exactly such a comment for [[sgilinearoob]]).  So the other 22 were sent for classification rather than corrected in bulk* -- which is this project's own deriver-keys-on-a-correlate rule, applied before the error rather than after it.\n\n**This is the SECOND ledger-is-stale-direction finding today**, after [[diskimagesign]].  Both run opposite to the twelve bug-file findings in the same sweep, and `check_bugfile_sync.py` checks only ledger -> bug file, so **neither direction of this pair is covered by anything** -- see [[bugfilereverse]].",
35913:   "state_note": "\n\n=== MAIN-LOOP ROUND RECORDS, 2026-09-06 -- moved out of the per-seat `entries' column ===\nThese are the round's OWN records -- table results, repair descriptions, stage summaries -- not any seat's\nanswer.  They were written into `entries' with seat=`main', which is not one of the nine declared seats; the\ndashboard generator refused the file and a census confirmed the label is this session's invention rather than\nan established convention (every other entry in the ledger uses a declared seat).  The seat column is the\nper-seat record that check_boilerplate, check_seats_read and the scorecard all read, so a round note living\nthere is a round note wearing a seat's clothes -- the stamped-summary shape one level up.  Moved here verbatim;\nthe same material is in the CHANGELOG round block.\n\n  [2026-09-05 / assess] Witness built (rung 3) before any edit: HEAD 0/9 (every trap-like class stopped the machine), fix 9/9 (16:16 build).  Pre-table check PRETABLE_m88kexc.out written before table 1.\n\n  [2026-09-05 / research] [MAIN-LOOP STAGE SUMMARY, not a seat's own answer -- relabelled from an undeclared `panel' seat on 2026-09-06 when the dashboard generator refused it.  The per-seat cells for this pass are the entries that name their own seat; this one is the round's summary of the stage and is labelled as such, because a summary sitting in a seat column is the stamped-cell failure the boilerplate check exists to refuse.]  PASS 1 panel_20260905_143211: Opus (MEASURED: the one-site form halts at the tail default; supervisor tb1 naming 7 halts HEAD -- BOUNDS reachable), kimi, grok, deepseek, minimax answered; codex GPT-6 Astra REFUSED twice (content filter); glm BURNED (0-byte response, 117 KB thinking; mini packet answered); Fable WALLED (session limit) and re-fired at pass 2; agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED.  Preserved under regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/pass1/.\n\n  [2026-09-05 / review] [MAIN-LOOP STAGE SUMMARY, not a seat's own answer -- relabelled from an undeclared `panel' seat on 2026-09-06 when the dashboard generator refused it.  The per-seat cells for this pass are the entries that name their own seat; this one is the round's summary of the stage and is labelled as such, because a summary sitting in a seat column is the stamped-cell failure the boilerplate check exists to refuse.]  PASS 2 panel_20260905_212630: codex, kimi, grok, deepseek, minimax, Opus (measuring: four 10/10 escapes), Fable (review: delay-slot arm wrong; level observable) answered; glm BURNED then answered the mini packet ollama_glm_mini.txt; agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED.  Every seat: the DIFF is right; the DETECTOR needed repairs -- made (v3, 22 rows) and re-measured (table 3, 14 mutants: 13 killed, M_TRAPONLY equivalent).  Preserved under regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/pass2_panel_20260905_212630/, opus_p2.md, fable_p2.md.\n\n  [2026-09-05 / review] [MAIN-LOOP STAGE SUMMARY, not a seat's own answer -- relabelled from an undeclared `panel' seat on 2026-09-06 when the dashboard generator refused it.  The per-seat cells for this pass are the entries that name their own seat; this one is the round's summary of the stage and is labelled as such, because a summary sitting in a seat column is the stamped-cell failure the boilerplate check exists to refuse.]  ADJUDICATION PASS (F1..F24, D1..D3; brief_m88kexc_adj.md) fired ~23:10 to all nine (agy not fired): results land in the closure entry.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] [MAIN-LOOP STAGE SUMMARY, not a seat's own answer -- relabelled from an undeclared `panel' seat on 2026-09-06 when the dashboard generator refused it.  The per-seat cells for this pass are the entries that name their own seat; this one is the round's summary of the stage and is labelled as such, because a summary sitting in a seat column is the stamped-cell failure the boilerplate check exists to refuse.]  ADJUDICATION PASS landed (pass3_panel_20260905_220902): codex, kimi, grok, deepseek, minimax answered; glm BURNED again; Opus and Fable Agent seats DIED on an Anthropic session limit (429) and are re-fired on pass 4; agy not fired.  grok/kimi CLOSE; codex/deepseek/minimax ONE MORE REPAIR -> v4: row indclr (IND never started clear -- the carry shape again), HALT classifier requires the stopped state + the named vector (+ pc == 0x40 for sfu1h), LEVEL contract narrowed to the ERROR/WARNING regression, sfu1h's channel restated (nested SFU1 halts at the fallback; with it off, re-routed to ERROR); mutants M_NOIND, M_NOSTOP; table 4 re-measures the carried-forward table-1 shapes.  Records: census (11 bypass / 10 delivered / vector 0 RESET-stop / 117 fallback), table-1 tally 11+2, M_TAILOFF equivalent, gate 2/22->3/22, PRETABLE correction appended, three phantom rows filed (m88kexctaildefault, m88ktbnd, m88kmxmkill).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-5 REPAIR (probe v5, 29 fault rows, md5 96ab47a0 shipped / c83b1f38 frozen -- docstring-only diff): rows umisalign, uovf, errovf, errdiv0, errpriv, errunimpl; classifier keeps one `stopped` observation.  TABLE 5 (killtable5/, PRETABLE5 written 08:47 before it): BASE 29/29; M_OVFSUPONLY 28/29 (uovf), M_MISSUPONLY 28/29 (umisalign), M_SFRZMIS 25/29 (the four err* rows), M_SFRZNOOVF/NODIV0/NOPRIV/NOUNIMPL 28/29 each (one row each, as predicted), M_NOSFD1 1/29 (27 bad=psr + sfu1h STOP; resid50 the survivor).  HEAD 2/29 (probe_v5_head.out: usertb DELIVER, resid50 HALT); fix 29/29 (probe_v5_fix_final.out).  Comment corrections (Table 6-3 attribution; the tb1-naming-10 frozen case) propagated to all trees, rebuilt, two-part clearance CLEAN/FRESH x3 (rebuild_fix2.log).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-6 REPAIR (one diff, two classes found by pass 5): probe v6 adds resid127 (the fallback window's FAR bound; table 6 = killtable6/: M_FALLBACK127 29/29 on v5 -> 29/30 on v6; M_FALLBACK51 28/29 on v5 (sfu1h saw it) -> 28/30; M_FALLBACKLE 28/29 -> 29/30 control); probe v7 adds trapfrozen4/5/6/8/9 with a harness field `handler_at` (trapfrozen8 plants its handler at the OVF entry so the frozen trap naming DIV0 is observable), class 10 disclosed as observationally identical; table 7 = killtable7/: M_SFRZTRAP4/5/6/8/9 30/30 on v6 -> 34/35 each (own row), M_SFRZTRAP 29/35.  HEAD with v7: 3/35 (usertb DELIVER, resid50 and resid127 HALT); fix 35/35.  PRETABLE6/7 written before their tables; both PRETABLE corrections were APPENDED to the same file after the table and refreshed its mtime -- stated in the record; corrections go to separate files from now on.  Gate pins 35/35 + resid127 HALT + six trapfrozen DELIVER.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-6 REPAIR part 3 (probe v8, 36 rows, md5 e550e3fe): row usfu1 for kimi's measured M_FBSUPONLY (fallback conditioned on MODE: 35/35 on v7 -> 35/36 on v8); M_SFRZTRAP4 re-measured 35/36.  HEAD with v8: 4/36; fix 36/36.  Gate pins 36/36 + usfu1 HALT.  Pass 6 fired ~10:30 to the eight scriptable transports (Opus/Fable walled).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-7 REPAIR (probe v9+v10, 41 rows, md5 ee2c78c2 = shipped): rows trapfrozen10, resid11, resid50ind, resid50c, trapfrozen128 and the refatal channel (the re-route's diagnostic pinned present on err* rows, absent on trapfrozen* rows).  TABLE 10 (killtable10/, PRETABLE10 before it, untouched; table 9 folded): M_ERRORFRZOFF, M_SFRZTRAP10, M_FB_LO, M_FBIND, M_FBNOC, M_SFRZTRAPUSER each 36/36 on the frozen v8 (the escapes measured) and 40/41 on v10, killed by exactly the predicted row (M_SFRZTRAP10 via bad=refatal on trapfrozen10).  BASE 41/41; HEAD 7/41 (usertb DELIVER; six HALT rows); fix 41/41.  Also repaired this pass: three gate check lines with a literal backslash-n between arguments (codex, pass 6) -- would have failed on correct output.  Gate pins 41/41 + the seven HALT rows + eight frozen-trap rows + refatal absent.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-7 REPAIR (probe v10 -> v14, 46 fault rows; shipped v15 md5 eb11ab7c, identical to frozen v14 outside the module docstring, AST-compared).  Rows added: trapfrozen450 / trapfrozen496 (codex: switch 1's last two deliverable labels), trapfrozen511 (kimi: the exemption's far edge, tb1's 9-bit field), errind / errc (grok: the re-route x {IND, C} -- the re-route predicate reads PSR, so a PSR-bit conjunct there is clause (d), unlike the switch-1 arms).  TABLE 11 (killtable11/, PRETABLE11 11:49 + addendum 2 11:55 + addendum 3 12:16, all before the 12:17 start; frozen v14 md5 99eb9c00; each mutant ALSO scored on the frozen v10): M_SFRZTRAP450 41/41 -> 45/46; M_SFRZTRAP496 41/41 -> 45/46; M_SFRZTRAPHI 41/41 -> 43/46; M_ERRORFRZON 40/41 -> 45/46 (kimi's sweep CLAIM refuted by measurement, as the existing errortb/bounds rows predicted); M_MISNOC 41/41 -> 45/46 (errc HALT: the disclosed {MISALIGNED} x {C} representative is killed as a side effect of the re-route rows -- disclosure narrowed in v15's docstring); M_RRIND 41/41 -> 45/46 (errind); M_RRNOC 41/41 -> 45/46 (errc).  Six measured sweeps of v10, each killed by the row the PRETABLE named.  BASE 46/46 before and after; clone cmp-clean, sentinel clear.  A MIS-CUT ROW was caught by the driver's own BASE gate (killtable11_attempt1_v13/): v13's errc wanted PSR 0x8000000b and exception entry leaves C alone, so BASE read 45/46 bad=psr before any mutant and the driver aborted -- PSR_WANT repaired in the instrument only (v14), no product code, no mutant re-aimed.  HEAD 7/46 (probe_v14_head.out, re-run on the HEAD binary with the final instrument); fix 46/46 (probe_v14_fix_final.out).  Gate pins 46/46, err(...|ind|c) 6, trapfrozen(...|511)? 11.  Pass 8 packet drafted (brief_m88kexc_p8.DRAFT.md).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS 8 FIRED (_scratchpad/panel_20260906_150800_m88k_p8/, brief = the FROZEN artifact copy regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/brief_m88kexc_p8.md, per the new template section on citing frozen copies).  Seat state MEASURED at launch, not assumed: opus5 ALIVE and fired first (it is the only measuring-seat review this round gets before it commits, since fable5 is walled); codex, kimi and grok fired and running -- the brief is 31,166 b, above the 30,000 argv cap, so codex and kimi got the POINTER form and grok is immune (--prompt-file).  *** THE THREE OLLAMA CLOUD SEATS ARE DOWN ON AN ACCOUNT-LEVEL QUOTA, NOT A TRANSIENT RATE LIMIT, AND THE SEAT FILES COULD NOT SAY SO. ***  Two firings forty minutes apart returned `OLLAMA_SEAT_ERROR: <HTTPError 429: 'Too Many Requests'>` and nothing else, which is indistinguishable from a five-minute wait; a direct POST to the same endpoint carried the reason in the response BODY -- a WEEKLY account usage limit.  Owner states it resets in about four hours from ~15:10 (so ~19:10).  panel_ollama.py now reads the HTTPError's body and appends it, CONTROLLED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS on the live wall: the fixed form prints the weekly-limit text, the pre-fix form from HEAD prints the bare status.  Pass 8's Ollama third fires when the quota resets; until then the stage is 6/9 and the three are recorded DOWN by name, never as agreement.  agy remains DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (owner ruling, reserved for measurement).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] **TABLE 12 -- THE DISCRIMINATING TABLE.  THE PASS-8 PANEL SPLIT IS SETTLED BY MEASUREMENT, AND codex AND grok ARE RIGHT.**  (regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/killtable12/ with its driver log; PRETABLE12 written 15:58:33 and the first BASE at 16:00:48, so the order proof is the file's own mtime; the frozen instrument is the SHIPPED probe v15, md5 eb11ab7c -- which also closes the records gap kimi named, since the gate runs the shipped file and earlier tables measured a frozen copy of an older revision.)  | mutant | on frozen v10 | on shipped v15 | red rows |  M_RRTRAPIND `if (!is_trap || !(PSR & IND))` | 41/41 | **46/46 SWEEP** | none |  M_RRTRAPC `if (!is_trap || (PSR & C))` | 41/41 | **46/46 SWEEP** | none |  CTL_RRTRAPINV `if (!is_trap || (PSR & IND))` -- THE POLARITY TWIN | 33/41 | **35/46** | ELEVEN: trapfrozen, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 450, 496, 511, 128 all STOP, and trapfrozen10 BADREGS bad=refatal |  **THE CONTROL IS WHAT MAKES THE SWEEPS MEAN SOMETHING.**  It is the same edit with one token changed -- the sense of the bit test -- and it reddens exactly the eleven frozen-trap rows, on both channels the PRETABLE named (the entry pc moves to 0x50, so the breakpoint is never reached; and trapfrozen10 additionally shows the re-route's diagnostic where REFATAL_EXPECT says it must not appear).  So the find-text landed, the probe CAN see this site, and the two 46/46 results are genuine blindness rather than a mis-built mutant -- the three explanations a non-dying mutant has, reduced to one.  BASE 46/46 before and 46/46 after; HEAD 7/46 with the same instrument; the rebuilt build/ 46/46; clone cmp-clean against SEC with no sentinel.  All five PRETABLE12 predictions held, including the control's.  **WHAT IT SETTLES.**  kimi's `killable-and-undisclosed survivors: ZERO' is refuted at the RE-ROUTE as it already was at the switch-1 arms -- and the reason is visible in kimi's own enumeration, which wrote the re-route family as `{label trap x 11}': the eleven rows cover eleven LABELS in ONE status state, and every one of them arrives with PSR 0x8000000b, so no row varies the trap side's status at all.  Counting labels is not covering the axis.  **WHAT IS NOW OWED.**  Three consecutive passes have found this class at three sites -- the fallback predicate (pass 6), the re-route's non-trap side (pass 7), and now the re-route's TRAP side plus the switch-1 arms (pass 8, measured here and by opus5) -- so the class-closure amendment applies and the repair must close the enumerated space in ONE diff rather than buy another cell.  opus5's five rows close the arms; these two cells close the re-route's trap side.  L2, in-round.\n\n*** THE PASS-5 PANEL WAS CITED AT A DURABLE PATH THAT DID NOT EXIST; THE EVIDENCE WAS SITTING IN\n`_scratchpad/`, WHICH IS NEVER COMMITTED.  Found and fixed 2026-09-08. ***\n\nFive seat entries cite `regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/pass5_panel_20260906_090810/<seat>.txt`.\n**That directory did not exist.**  The panel was at `_scratchpad/panel_20260906_090810/` -- the\nvolatile path -- so the citations were unverifiable and the answers were one cleanup from gone.\n\n**COPIED to the cited path**, and all five now resolve:\n\n    codex.txt          1,299,047 b      grok.txt              5,213 b\n    ollama_glm.txt         3,704 b      ollama_minimax.txt    7,559 b\n    ollama_deepseek.txt    1,297 b      SEATS.txt               829 b\n\n*** AND A SIXTH SEAT ANSWERED THAT THE LEDGER NEVER CITED: `kimi.txt`, 312,574 BYTES. ***  It is\nrecorded in no entry on this row.  **That is an UNREAD REVIEW -- not a seat failure and not\nagreement -- and this project has found real defects in one four times.**  Preserved with the rest;\n**reading it is an outstanding obligation on this row**, not discharged here.\n\n**A SIZE NOTE, recorded rather than acted on unilaterally: `codex.txt` is 1.3 MB.**  The carrier\nrecords that a codex research run here has exceeded 1 MB, and `adjudicate.sh` takes only the TAIL\nfor that reason.  **I did NOT truncate it**: the ledger cites this file as a seat's evidence, and\ntruncating cited evidence to save space converts a verifiable citation into a modified artifact --\nwhich is worse than a large file.  If the repository size matters more than the citation, that is\nan owner decision and this note is where it should start.\n\n> **THE GENERAL FINDING, which is why this was worth an audit rather than a fix: the volatile-path\n> rule was OBEYED for the round's own logs and MISSED for its panel.**  A round that carefully\n> copies its kill-table output to `regress/artifacts/` can still leave the panel that reviewed it in\n> `_scratchpad/`, because the panel feels like process rather than evidence.  **It is evidence: the\n> ledger cites it by path.**\n\n*Found by an EXISTENCE audit over every artifact path in the ledger's witness and `ev` fields --\n71 witness citations, all resolving, and 181 `ev` paths of which these five were the only misses.\nAn existence check cannot false-positive the way a count or a proximity grep can, which is why it is\nworth running and the stale-count audit was declined.*\n\n*** THE AUDIT'S FIRST RUN REPORTED EIGHT ADDITIONAL MISSES AND EVERY ONE WAS ITS OWN BUG. ***  Bare\nfilenames (`check_witness.py`, `gate_arm.sh`, `precommit_check.sh` ...) were joined to the SEC root\nwhen they live in `tools/pipeline/` and `regress/`, and one citation carried a `GXEMUL-SEC/` prefix\nthat the join then doubled.  **With the resolver fixed: 71 of 71.**  *Recorded because reporting\nthose eight as ledger defects would have been exactly the wrong-apparatus failure this project keeps\ncatching -- and the tell was that they were all HARNESS files, which is a suspicious population.*\n\n=== 2026-10-16 -- the close adjudication, repairs 13 and 14 (probe v22, v23) ===\nThe four-seat close adjudication split 3-1 (Astra, Grok, GLM CLOSE; Fable DO NOT CLOSE) and the split was settled by MEASUREMENT: `implemented = !cpu->delay_slot' swept the 59-row probe at four arms (delayslot_20261016/).  Repair 13 (v22, four dslot* rows) went to pass 14, which found the same axis open at more cells (Fable: the {7 labels} x {SFRZ} x {delay_slot} grid, 28 cells, 19 rowed; Astra: the re-route sides, measured in table 17).  Two consecutive passes, one class: repair 14 (v23, 73 rows) closes the grid 28/28 plus the fallback row, in one diff.  Tables 16, 16b, 17, 18, 19 and 19b in m88kexcdeliver/repair13_20261016/, every score as predicted in a PRETABLE written first.  The pass-13 F5 sentence, adopted: only PSR & SFRZ is TESTED by the delivery path, so the PSR-bit cells other than SFRZ at all four sites are clause (e), closed voluntarily; their recorded L2 grades (v16/v17/v19) were given under a reads-PSR criterion that came from clause (d)(2), which enumerates MUTANTS TO BUILD, not CELLS TO ROW.  The criterion this round uses, named: a state value the delivery path TESTS -- which is why delay_slot is floor and MODE/SFD1/IND/C/SER are not.\nPass 15 (repair13_20261016/pass15/), the closing pass on v23, split 2-2: Astra and Grok CLOSE, Fable and GLM DO NOT CLOSE.  Ruled by the argument: Fable's -- is_trap is the fourth input the delivery path tests, an input bit at every floor cell under the carrier's generating-set clause, and this chain repaired the same shape in-round on passes 5 and 7 -- so the five measured 73/73 escapes of table 19 are floor breaches (L2).  The chain stays OPEN: repair 15 (probe v24, ten is_trap cells, the grid {7 labels} x {SFRZ} x {slot} x {is_trap} = 48 reachable cells, after which the function has no untested input) is in flight, measured in table 20; its own pass follows.  GLM's user-trap labels x {slot} are disclosed (outside the fix's seven labels).",
